// Syllabus checklist from the Embedded Tracker. Row id = section:topic:row (same as the tracker).
export const SYL = [{"id":"elec","num":"0","name":"Electronics Foundations","month":"Month 1","desc":"Circuit theorems, MOSFET behaviour, op-amps, CMOS at transistor level, digital timing, converters, power. One hour a day, every concept simulated in LTspice before you believe it.","topics":[{"n":"Circuit theorems","subs":[{"n":"Ohm's law and power","q":"A 5 V rail drives 220 ohm. Find current, and power dissipated. Which resistor package (0402 / 0603 / 0805) survives it?"},{"n":"Passive sign convention","q":"Label a resistor and a battery in the same loop. Which one absorbs power and which delivers it? Show the sign of P in each."},{"n":"KVL - conservation of energy","t":"key","q":"12 V source, 3 k ohm and 6 k ohm in series. Write the KVL loop equation and solve for I and the voltage across each resistor."},{"n":"Mesh analysis and supermesh","q":"Two meshes share a 2 mA current source. Write the supermesh equation plus the constraint equation, and solve for both mesh currents."},{"n":"KCL - conservation of charge","t":"key","q":"A node has 3 mA in, 1.2 mA out on one branch, and an unknown branch. Find the unknown. Then state why charge cannot pile up at the node."},{"n":"Nodal analysis and supernode","q":"Two nodes are bridged by a floating 5 V source. Write the supernode KCL equation and the constraint, and solve."},{"n":"Voltage and current dividers","q":"Design a divider that turns 12 V into 3.0 V for an ADC input. Pick real E24 values, then compute the error caused by a 1 M ohm ADC input impedance."},{"n":"Thevenin equivalent","t":"key","q":"Reduce a network of 3 resistors and 2 sources to V_th and R_th. Verify by simulating both the original and the equivalent under the same load."},{"n":"R_th with dependent sources","q":"A circuit contains a voltage-controlled current source. You cannot just null the sources. Apply the test-source method: find R_th = V_test / I_test."},{"n":"Norton equivalent and source transformation","q":"Convert your Thevenin answer above to Norton. Prove I_N = V_th / R_th by shorting the terminals in simulation."},{"n":"Superposition - and where it fails","t":"key","q":"Two sources drive one resistor. Find the resistor voltage by superposition. Now try to find its power the same way. Show numerically why superposition gives the wrong answer for power."},{"n":"Millman's theorem","q":"Three parallel branches each have a source and a series resistor. Use Millman to find the common node voltage in one step."},{"n":"Maximum power transfer","q":"For R_th = 50 ohm, find R_L for maximum power and the efficiency at that point. Why do power supplies deliberately violate this rule?"},{"n":"Delta-Wye transformation","q":"Convert a 3-resistor delta into its wye equivalent, then use it to collapse a bridge circuit that has no series/parallel simplification."}]},{"n":"Transients and AC","subs":[{"n":"RC time constant","t":"key","q":"R=10 k ohm, C=100 nF. Find tau. How long to reach 63.2%? To settle within 1%? Simulate and mark tau, 2tau, 5tau on the plot."},{"n":"RL time constant","q":"A 10 mH inductor with 4.7 ohm series. Find tau = L/R. Sketch current on switch-on and voltage at switch-off - explain the inductive kick."},{"n":"RLC damping and resonance","q":"L=1 mH, C=100 nF. Find f_res and Q for R = 1 ohm, 63 ohm, and 1 k ohm. Classify each as under-, critically-, or over-damped. Simulate all three."},{"n":"Phasors and impedance","q":"At 10 kHz, find the impedance of a 100 nF cap and a 10 mH inductor. Write each as Z = R + jX. Which dominates?"},{"n":"AC power and power factor","t":"aw","q":"A load draws 2 A at 230 V with a 30 deg lag. Find real, reactive, and apparent power, and the power factor."},{"n":"Bode plots, poles, roll-off","t":"key","q":"An RC low-pass has R=1 k ohm, C=10 nF. Find f_c. Sketch the Bode magnitude. What is the attenuation at 10xf_c, and what is the phase at f_c?"}]},{"n":"Diodes and BJT","subs":[{"n":"PN junction physics","q":"Explain the depletion region, built-in potential, and what changes under forward vs reverse bias. Why does forward voltage fall as temperature rises?"},{"n":"Diode I-V and knee voltage","q":"Simulate a 1N4148 I-V sweep. Read V_f at 1 mA and at 100 mA. Why isn't the 'knee' a single fixed number?"},{"n":"Zener as voltage reference","q":"Design a 5.1 V Zener reference from a 12 V rail supplying 5 mA of load. Size the series resistor. What happens when the load disconnects?"},{"n":"Rectifiers and ripple","t":"key","q":"Full-wave bridge, 12 V AC, 100 mA load, 50 Hz. Size the filter cap for under 1 V ripple. Simulate and measure actual ripple against your calculation."},{"n":"Schottky diodes","q":"Compare V_f and reverse recovery of 1N4007 vs 1N5819 in simulation. Explain why SMPS designs need the Schottky."},{"n":"Flyback / freewheeling diode","t":"key","q":"Switch a relay coil with a MOSFET, no flyback diode. Simulate the drain voltage at turn-off. Add the diode. Explain the number you saw before."},{"n":"BJT operating regions","t":"key","q":"For a 2N2222 with V_CC=5 V, R_C=1 k ohm: find I_B for cutoff, active, and saturation. State V_CE in each region."},{"n":"BJT as a switch","q":"Drive a 100 mA load from a 3.3 V GPIO using an NPN. Size the base resistor for hard saturation (overdrive factor 10). What is V_CE(sat) and the resulting power loss?"}]},{"n":"MOSFET","subs":[{"n":"NMOS vs PMOS structure","q":"Draw both symbols with source, drain, gate, body. For each, state where the source sits relative to the supply rails in a switching circuit."},{"n":"V_gs, V_th, V_ds","t":"key","q":"An IRLZ44N has V_th ~ 1-2 V. You drive its gate from a 3.3 V MCU. Is it fully on? Read R_ds(on) vs V_gs from the datasheet and justify."},{"n":"Triode vs saturation region","t":"key","q":"State the boundary condition. For V_gs=5 V, V_th=2 V: at what V_ds does the device leave triode? Which region does a switch operate in, and which does an amplifier?"},{"n":"I-V characteristic family","q":"Sweep V_ds from 0-10 V for V_gs = 2, 3, 4, 5 V. Plot the family. Mark the pinch-off locus. Identify the operating region at V_gs=4 V, V_ds=1 V."},{"n":"Transconductance g_m","t":"aw","q":"From your I-V family, extract g_m = deltaI_d/deltaV_gs at a fixed V_ds. Why does g_m matter for an amplifier but not for a switch?"},{"n":"Channel-length modulation","q":"In saturation, I_d still rises slowly with V_ds. Explain the physical cause and how lambda models it. Which region does this hurt: switching or amplification?"},{"n":"Body effect","t":"aw","q":"Explain why V_th rises when the source sits above the body. Where does this bite you - high-side or low-side switching?"},{"n":"R_ds(on) and conduction loss","t":"key","q":"R_ds(on) = 22 m ohm at V_gs=10 V but 40 m ohm at 4.5 V. At 3 A, compute conduction loss in both cases. Now compute the junction temperature rise with R_thetaJA = 62 degC/W."},{"n":"Gate capacitance and gate charge","q":"From a datasheet, read C_iss, C_oss, C_rss and Q_g. If your driver sources 20 mA, how long does the gate take to charge? Is that fast enough at 100 kHz?"},{"n":"Miller plateau and switching loss","t":"key","q":"Simulate a MOSFET turn-on and capture the flat region in V_gs. Explain what the plateau is, and why switching loss is highest there."},{"n":"Why a gate driver exists","q":"Your MCU pin sources 8 mA. Your MOSFET needs 30 nC. Compute the switching time. At 50 kHz, estimate switching loss. Now justify the gate driver."},{"n":"Shoot-through and dead-time","t":"key","q":"In a half-bridge, both FETs briefly conduct. Explain the failure. Compute the minimum dead-time from the datasheet's t_d(off) and t_d(on)."},{"n":"Low-side vs high-side switching","q":"Draw both. For high-side NMOS, explain why you need a gate voltage above V+ and how a bootstrap capacitor supplies it."},{"n":"Logic-level vs standard MOSFET","t":"key","q":"You drove an IRF540 gate with 3.3 V and it got hot. Read its R_ds(on) vs V_gs curve. Explain the mistake and name the correct part."},{"n":"Body diode","q":"Identify the body diode's orientation in an NMOS. In a half-bridge, when does it conduct, and why does its reverse recovery matter?"},{"n":"Thermal: R_thetaJA, SOA, derating","q":"A SOT-23 FET dissipates 0.4 W with R_thetaJA = 250 degC/W at 25 degC ambient. Find T_J. Is it within a 150 degC limit? What changes at 70 degC ambient?"}]},{"n":"Op-amps","subs":[{"n":"Ideal op-amp assumptions","q":"List the four ideal assumptions. For each, name the real-world parameter that breaks it and give a typical LM358 value."},{"n":"Golden rules and virtual ground","t":"key","q":"Explain why the inverting input sits at 0 V in an inverting amp even though it isn't connected to ground. Then derive G = -R_f/R_in from the two golden rules."},{"n":"Inverting amplifier","q":"Design G = -10 with input impedance >= 10 k ohm. Pick E24 values. Simulate an AC sweep and confirm the gain and the -3 dB point."},{"n":"Non-inverting amplifier","q":"Design G = +11. Why can this configuration never have gain below 1? Simulate and verify."},{"n":"Voltage follower / buffer","t":"key","q":"A 100 k ohm sensor divider feeds an ADC with 50 k ohm input impedance. Compute the error. Insert a buffer and recompute. State the buffer's actual job."},{"n":"Summing and difference amplifiers","q":"Build a 3-input summing amp with weights 1, 2, 4 - a crude 3-bit DAC. Simulate all 8 codes and confirm the output steps."},{"n":"Instrumentation amplifier and CMRR","t":"key","q":"A 2 mV bridge signal rides on a 2.5 V common-mode. With CMRR = 90 dB, compute the common-mode error at the output. Why can't a single difference amp do this job well?"},{"n":"Integrator and differentiator","q":"Feed a square wave into an integrator - predict the output shape, then simulate. Now try the differentiator with a noisy input. Explain why differentiators are avoided."},{"n":"Comparator vs op-amp","t":"key","q":"Use an LM358 as a comparator and simulate the output near the trip point. Now use an LM393. Explain the difference in propagation delay and output stage."},{"n":"Hysteresis / Schmitt trigger","t":"key","q":"Design a Schmitt trigger with trip points at 1.5 V and 2.0 V from a 3.3 V rail. Compute both thresholds from your resistor values, then verify in simulation with a noisy ramp."},{"n":"Transimpedance amplifier","t":"aw","q":"A photodiode outputs 10 uA. Design a TIA for 1 V out. What sets the bandwidth, and why do you need the feedback capacitor?"},{"n":"Real non-idealities","t":"key","q":"For an LM358: find V_os, I_b, CMRR, PSRR, slew rate, and GBW in the datasheet. With G=100, what is the DC output error caused by V_os alone?"},{"n":"Slew rate and GBW product","q":"GBW = 1 MHz. What bandwidth do you get at G=100? Separately: with SR = 0.5 V/us, what is the largest undistorted 10 kHz sine amplitude?"},{"n":"Active filters - Sallen-Key","t":"key","q":"Design a 2nd-order Butterworth low-pass at f_c = 1 kHz. Simulate; confirm the -3 dB point and the -40 dB/decade roll-off."},{"n":"Op-amp stability with capacitive load","t":"aw","q":"Drive a 1 nF load directly from an op-amp output and simulate the step response. Explain the ringing, then fix it with an isolation resistor."}]},{"n":"ADC and DAC","subs":[{"n":"Quantization, LSB, resolution","t":"key","q":"12-bit ADC, V_ref = 3.3 V. Find the LSB size in mV. Your sensor outputs 0-50 mV - how many codes do you actually use? What do you do about it?"},{"n":"Quantization noise and SQNR","q":"Derive/state SQNR = 6.02N + 1.76 dB. Compute it for 8-bit and 12-bit. Simulate both quantizations in Python and plot the error."},{"n":"ENOB","t":"key","q":"A datasheet claims 12 bits with SINAD = 68 dB. Compute ENOB. Explain to an interviewer why ENOB is the honest number and 12 is marketing."},{"n":"SNR, SINAD, THD, SFDR","t":"aw","q":"Define each. Which one includes harmonics? Which one includes noise? Which is the single most useful figure for a sensor front-end?"},{"n":"INL and DNL","q":"Explain both. Which one causes missing codes? Which one is fatal for a control loop, and why?"},{"n":"Sampling theorem and Nyquist","t":"key","q":"You sample a 3 kHz sine at 5 kSPS. What apparent frequency appears? Simulate it in Python and plot the alias."},{"n":"Anti-alias filter","t":"key","q":"You sample at 1 kSPS. Your signal has content at 700 Hz. Design the anti-alias filter: state f_c and the order needed, and justify it."},{"n":"Oversampling and decimation","q":"You have a 12-bit ADC and need 14 bits. How many samples must you average, and what is the required condition on the noise for this to work?"},{"n":"SAR ADC architecture","t":"key","q":"Walk through a 4-bit SAR conversion for V_in = 0.6 | V_ref, bit by bit. How many clock cycles does an N-bit SAR need?"},{"n":"Sigma-delta ADC","q":"Explain oversampling plus noise shaping in one paragraph. When do you pick sigma-delta over SAR? What do you give up?"},{"n":"Acquisition time vs source impedance","t":"key","q":"Your ADC has a 5 pF sampling cap and needs 0.5 LSB settling at 12 bits. Your sensor has 100 k ohm output impedance. Compute the minimum sampling time. This is the #1 real ADC bug - explain why."},{"n":"V_ref and ratiometric measurement","q":"Your supply sags from 3.3 V to 3.1 V. Show how a ratiometric resistive sensor reading stays correct while an absolute voltage reading does not."},{"n":"DAC: R-2R and PWM+RC","q":"Design a PWM DAC: 8-bit at 10 kHz PWM. Size the RC filter for under 10 mV ripple. What settling time did you just buy?"}]},{"n":"Digital logic","subs":[{"n":"Number systems and Gray code","q":"Convert 0xB7 to binary and decimal. Then write the 3-bit Gray sequence and explain why a rotary encoder uses it instead of binary."},{"n":"Two's complement","t":"key","q":"Represent -37 in 8-bit two's complement. Add it to +37 and show the result. Then show an addition that overflows, and state how hardware detects it."},{"n":"Universal gates","t":"key","q":"Build XOR using only NAND gates. Draw it. How many did you need? Now do it with NOR only."},{"n":"Boolean algebra and De Morgan","t":"key","q":"Simplify F = A'B + AB + AB'. Then apply De Morgan to (A + B | C)' and verify both with a truth table."},{"n":"K-map minimization","t":"key","q":"Minimize F(A,B,C,D) = summ(0,1,2,5,8,9,10) with don't-cares at (3,7). Show the groupings and the final SOP expression."},{"n":"MUX, DEMUX, encoder, decoder","q":"Design a 4-to-1 MUX from gates. Then implement F = A'B + AC using only that MUX - no extra logic."},{"n":"Half adder and full adder","t":"key","q":"Build a full adder from two half adders plus one gate. Then chain four into a ripple-carry adder and state its worst-case delay in gate delays."},{"n":"Hazards and glitches","t":"aw","q":"Show a static-1 hazard on a K-map. Explain the fix (redundant prime implicant). Why does this matter for combinational logic driving a clock?"},{"n":"Latch vs flip-flop","t":"key","q":"State the one-line difference. Draw a D latch and a D flip-flop, and their waveforms for the same D and clock. Why does synthesis warn about inferred latches?"},{"n":"D, JK, T flip-flops","q":"Write the characteristic table for each. Convert a D flip-flop into a T flip-flop using one extra gate."},{"n":"Shift registers","q":"Design a 4-bit SIPO shift register. Now explain how a 74HC595 lets you drive 8 outputs from 3 MCU pins."},{"n":"Synchronous vs ripple counters","t":"key","q":"Design a mod-6 synchronous counter. Then explain why a ripple counter's outputs glitch, and why that disqualifies it from clocking anything."},{"n":"Moore vs Mealy FSM","t":"key","q":"Design a '101' sequence detector both ways. Compare state count and output timing. Which one responds a cycle earlier, and what is the cost?"},{"n":"State encoding: binary vs one-hot","q":"Encode a 5-state FSM both ways. Compare flip-flop count against next-state logic depth. Which does an FPGA prefer, and why?"},{"n":"Memory types","t":"aw","q":"Build a table: SRAM, DRAM, EEPROM, Flash - volatility, cost/bit, access time, endurance. Which one is your MCU's RAM, and which is its program store?"}]},{"n":"CMOS at transistor level","subs":[{"n":"CMOS inverter","t":"key","q":"Draw it with PMOS on top, NMOS below. Trace what conducts for input 0 and input 1. Why is static current ~zero in both states?"},{"n":"Voltage transfer characteristic","t":"key","q":"Simulate the VTC. Mark V_IL, V_IH, V_OL, V_OH. Compute NM_H and NM_L. What does a narrow noise margin mean physically?"},{"n":"Sizing W_p/W_n","q":"Hole mobility is ~2-3x lower than electron mobility. Derive the PMOS width ratio for a symmetric VTC. Simulate at ratio 1 and at ratio 2.5 and compare."},{"n":"Rise/fall time and propagation delay","t":"key","q":"Simulate the inverter with a 10 fF load. Measure t_pHL and t_pLH. Now double the load - what happened to both, and why?"},{"n":"Static vs dynamic vs short-circuit power","t":"key","q":"Write P_dyn = alpha | C | V^2 | f. At 100 MHz, C=50 fF, V=1.8 V, alpha=0.2 - compute it. Then explain the two other power terms and which dominates in a sleeping MCU."},{"n":"CMOS NAND and NOR at transistor level","t":"key","q":"Draw both with 4 transistors each. State the PUN/PDN duality rule. Verify the NAND truth table in simulation."},{"n":"Why NAND is preferred over NOR","t":"key","q":"Both use 4 transistors. Explain why NOR is slower in CMOS - reason about the series PMOS stack and mobility."},{"n":"Transmission gate","q":"Draw a PMOS+NMOS transmission gate. Why do you need both? Build a 2-to-1 MUX from two of them."},{"n":"Fan-out and loading","q":"An inverter drives 8 identical gates. Estimate the delay increase. What is the fix used in real chips for large fan-out?"},{"n":"Tri-state buffer","q":"Draw a tri-state output stage. Explain 'high impedance' at transistor level. Where is this used on a real bus?"}]},{"n":"Digital timing","subs":[{"n":"Setup time, hold time, clock-to-Q","t":"key","q":"Define all three on a waveform. Which one is a constraint on the launching path and which on the capturing path?"},{"n":"Maximum frequency calculation","t":"key","q":"t_co = 0.8 ns, t_logic = 4.2 ns, t_su = 0.5 ns, skew = 0.3 ns. Compute f_max. Now the design must hit 250 MHz - what do you change?"},{"n":"Setup vs hold violation - and the fixes","t":"key","q":"A design fails setup. Does slowing the clock fix it? Now it fails hold. Does slowing the clock fix that? Explain both answers and give the correct fix for each."},{"n":"Clock skew and jitter","q":"Define both. Show a case where positive skew helps setup but hurts hold. Why does a clock tree exist?"},{"n":"Metastability and MTBF","t":"key","q":"An asynchronous button feeds a flip-flop. Explain what physically happens on a setup violation. Then state what MTBF depends on."},{"n":"Two-flop synchronizer","t":"key","q":"Draw it. Explain why two flops and not one - and why not three. What does the first flop actually buy you?"},{"n":"Clock domain crossing","t":"key","q":"You must pass an 8-bit value from a 10 MHz to a 100 MHz domain. Why is a two-flop synchronizer per bit wrong? Name the two correct approaches."}]},{"n":"Power electronics","subs":[{"n":"LDO fundamentals","t":"key","q":"An AMS1117 drops 5 V to 3.3 V at 500 mA. Compute the power dissipated and the efficiency. With R_thetaJA = 60 degC/W, find T_J at 25 degC ambient. Is it usable?"},{"n":"LDO dropout, I_q, PSRR","q":"Your battery falls to 3.4 V and the LDO must give 3.3 V. Which LDO parameter decides if this works? For a sleeping node at 5 uA, which parameter dominates instead?"},{"n":"Buck converter operation","t":"key","q":"12 V in, 3.3 V out. Compute the duty cycle. With L = 22 uH at 500 kHz and 1 A load, compute the inductor ripple current. Simulate and compare."},{"n":"CCM vs DCM","q":"At what load current does your buck above enter DCM? What changes about the output ripple and the control loop when it does?"},{"n":"Output cap ESR and ripple","q":"Your 1 A buck has 200 mA ripple current. With a cap of ESR = 30 m ohm, compute the ESR-driven ripple. Compare with the capacitive term. Which dominates?"},{"n":"LDO vs SMPS trade-off","t":"have","q":"You used a Hi-Link SMPS and a TP4056. Build the decision table: efficiency, noise, cost, BOM size, EMI. When would you deliberately choose the worse-efficiency part?"},{"n":"Decoupling capacitors","t":"key","q":"Why 100 nF at every VDD pin plus one 10 uF bulk? Explain what each supplies and over what frequency range. Why does placement matter more than value?"},{"n":"ESR, ESL, self-resonance","q":"A 100 nF 0603 MLCC self-resonates around 20-30 MHz. Above that it is inductive. Explain the consequence, and why you place two different values in parallel."},{"n":"Reverse polarity and TVS protection","q":"Design reverse-polarity protection for a 12 V input using a P-channel MOSFET. Why is this better than a series diode? Then add a TVS and pick its standoff voltage."},{"n":"Li-ion CC/CV charging","t":"have","q":"You used the TP4056. Draw the full CC/CV curve with the termination point. What sets the charge current, and what happens if you charge a cold cell?"}]}]},{"id":"c","num":"1","name":"C for Embedded","month":"Woven M1-M5","desc":"The language you will be judged in. Not CS-level C - the dialect that touches registers, survives an optimizer, and never calls malloc.","topics":[{"n":"Types and storage","subs":[{"n":"stdint.h fixed-width types","q":"Why is `unsigned int reg` wrong in a register map but `uint32_t reg` right? Give a case where `int` differs in width between two compilers."},{"n":"Integer promotion traps","t":"key","q":"`uint8_t a=200, b=100; if (a+b > 255)` - does this fire? Explain what type a+b actually is. Now: `if (-1 < sizeof(int))` - does it fire? Why not?"},{"n":"static - two different meanings","t":"key","q":"Explain `static` at file scope vs inside a function. Write one example of each and state what changes about linkage and lifetime."},{"n":"extern and linkage","q":"You defined a global in main.c and want it in uart.c. Show the correct header pattern. What breaks if you write the definition in the header?"},{"n":"volatile","t":"key","q":"Write a spin-wait on a status register without `volatile`. Compile at -O2, read the disassembly, and show the infinite loop the compiler created. Then fix it."},{"n":"volatile - the three real cases","q":"Name the three situations that require volatile: memory-mapped register, ISR-shared variable, and one more. Then state what volatile does NOT give you (hint: atomicity)."},{"n":"const - pointer vs pointee","t":"key","q":"Distinguish `const char *p`, `char * const p`, and `const char * const p`. Then explain when you would ever write `const volatile` and give a real register example."}]},{"n":"Pointers and memory","subs":[{"n":"Pointer arithmetic and decay","q":"`int a[5]; int *p = a;` - what is `sizeof(a)` vs `sizeof(p)`? What is `p+1` numerically? Explain array-to-pointer decay in one sentence."},{"n":"Function pointers","t":"key","q":"Write the type of a pointer to a function taking (uint8_t*, size_t) and returning int. Then build a 4-entry dispatch table and call through it."},{"n":"void* and generic containers","q":"Write a generic swap(void *a, void *b, size_t n). Why does it need the size argument when a C++ template would not?"},{"n":"Memory-mapped register access","t":"key","q":"Write the macro for a register at 0x40020014. Explain every part of `(*(volatile uint32_t *)0x40020014UL)` - including why the cast, why volatile, why UL."},{"n":"Memory sections: text, rodata, data, bss","t":"key","q":"For each of these, say which section it lands in: a const lookup table, a zero-initialized global, a `static int x = 5`, a string literal, a local variable."},{"n":"malloc, fragmentation, and why it's banned","t":"key","q":"Demonstrate heap fragmentation with an alloc/free pattern that leaves 10 KB free but cannot serve a 4 KB request. Now state the two embedded alternatives."},{"n":"Memory pools / fixed-block allocators","q":"Implement a fixed-block allocator: 32 blocks of 64 bytes, O(1) alloc and free, no fragmentation possible. Explain why fragmentation is structurally impossible here."},{"n":"restrict and inline","t":"aw","q":"What does `restrict` promise the compiler? Write a memcpy-style loop and explain the optimization it unlocks."}]},{"n":"Data layout","subs":[{"n":"Struct padding and alignment","t":"key","q":"`struct { uint8_t a; uint32_t b; uint8_t c; };` - what is sizeof on ARM? Reorder the fields to shrink it. Verify with offsetof."},{"n":"Packed structs and unaligned faults","t":"key","q":"You added __attribute__((packed)) to parse a wire protocol. On Cortex-M0 it HardFaults. Explain why, and give two ways to fix it."},{"n":"Unions and type punning","q":"Convert a float to its 4 raw bytes using a union. Then do it with a pointer cast. Explain why the union is the legal one."},{"n":"Bitfields for register maps","t":"key","q":"Write a bitfield struct for a UART control register. Then state the two portability traps (bit order and straddling) that make some codebases ban bitfields entirely."},{"n":"Endianness and wire format","t":"key","q":"Write byte-swap functions for 16- and 32-bit values. Your MCU is little-endian and the protocol is big-endian - where exactly in your driver does the swap belong?"},{"n":"Alignment for DMA buffers","q":"Why must a DMA buffer on Cortex-M7 be 32-byte aligned? Write the attribute that guarantees it."}]},{"n":"Bit manipulation","subs":[{"n":"Set, clear, toggle, test","t":"key","q":"Write all four as macros for bit n of a uint32_t. Then explain why every literal must be `1u` and not `1`."},{"n":"Multi-bit field read-modify-write","t":"key","q":"A register has a 3-bit MODE field at bits [6:4]. Write a function that sets it to a value without disturbing any other bit."},{"n":"Atomic vs non-atomic register writes","t":"key","q":"`GPIOA->ODR |= (1<<5)` is a read-modify-write. Show the race if an ISR touches the same port. Then rewrite it using BSRR and explain why that is atomic."}]},{"n":"Preprocessor and build","subs":[{"n":"Function-like macros and parenthesization","q":"`#define SQ(x) x*x` - evaluate SQ(2+3). Fix the macro. Then find the case your fix still gets wrong (hint: side effects)."},{"n":"do-while(0) idiom","t":"key","q":"Write a two-statement macro without do-while(0). Show the exact `if/else` that breaks. Then fix it and explain why do-while(0) and not braces alone."},{"n":"X-macros","q":"Use an X-macro to generate, from one list, both an enum of error codes and a matching string table. Show that adding an entry needs one line."},{"n":"static_assert","q":"Add a compile-time assertion that your packed protocol struct is exactly 12 bytes. Break it deliberately and read the error."},{"n":"Optimization levels vs volatile","t":"key","q":"Compile the same delay loop at -O0 and -O2. Diff the disassembly. Explain what -O2 legally removed and why."},{"n":"Size flags: gc-sections","q":"Build once normally, once with -ffunction-sections -fdata-sections -Wl,--gc-sections. Compare `size` output. Explain the mechanism."}]},{"n":"Correctness and safety","subs":[{"n":"Undefined behaviour","t":"key","q":"Name three UB cases you can hit in firmware. For signed overflow, show how -O2 can delete an overflow check you wrote."},{"n":"Strict aliasing","t":"key","q":"Write a float -> uint32 reinterpret via pointer cast. Explain why it is UB, and why the union version is not."},{"n":"Reentrancy and ISR-safe coding","t":"key","q":"List five things you must never do in an ISR. For each, state the failure mode. Then explain why printf is on the list."},{"n":"Critical sections and atomicity","t":"key","q":"A 32-bit counter is incremented in an ISR and read in main. On Cortex-M is the read atomic? Now make it a 64-bit counter - is it? Write the correct protection."},{"n":"Buffer safety","q":"Show a real overflow using strncpy's non-terminating behaviour. Rewrite with snprintf. State the return value trap in snprintf."},{"n":"Error-code design","q":"Design an error enum for a sensor driver. Show how a caller propagates it up three layers without exceptions and without losing the origin."},{"n":"MISRA C:2012","t":"aw","q":"Name three MISRA rules you already break. Run cppcheck --addon=misra on your own code and read the top 5 findings. What is a 'deviation record'?"}]},{"n":"Embedded idioms","subs":[{"n":"Ring buffer","t":"key","q":"Implement a ring buffer with power-of-two masking. Then make it lock-free for one producer (ISR) and one consumer (main) - prove no lock is needed."},{"n":"Driver API design","t":"key","q":"Design a UART driver API with an opaque handle. Show the header a caller sees and hide every register from them. Why does the opaque pointer matter for testing?"},{"n":"HAL abstraction seam","t":"key","q":"Split your sensor driver so the logic never touches a register directly. Now compile that logic for x86 and unit-test it with no board. This seam is the whole point - explain why."},{"n":"Callback / observer pattern","q":"Add a completion callback to your UART driver. Which context does the callback run in? What are you now forbidden from doing inside it?"},{"n":"FSM: switch-case vs dispatch table","q":"Implement a 5-state protocol parser both ways. Compare code size, speed, and readability. Which one scales to 40 states?"},{"n":"Fixed-point Q15/Q31","t":"key","q":"Represent 0.75 in Q15. Multiply two Q15 numbers - what intermediate type do you need and how do you rescale? When would you use this over a float?"},{"n":"Doxygen","q":"Document your ring buffer API with @brief, @param, @return, @note. Generate the HTML and read it as if you were a new team member."},{"n":"Embedded C++","t":"aw","q":"Name three C++ features banned in most firmware and why. Then name two worth having (RAII, constexpr) and what they cost you."}]},{"n":"State machines and event-driven design","subs":[{"n":"Event-driven vs input-driven","t":"key","q":"Your protocol parser polls a flag in a super-loop. Rewrite it event-driven: events arrive on a queue, the machine reacts. Measure CPU time and worst-case latency before and after."},{"n":"Run-to-completion semantics","t":"key","q":"Explain RTC. Why may a state machine never block inside a transition? Show what happens to the event queue when one does."},{"n":"Hierarchical state machines","t":"key","q":"Five states all handle a CANCEL event identically. Show the duplication in a flat FSM, then factor it into a superstate. Count the lines you deleted."},{"n":"Moore vs Mealy in code","q":"Implement the same detector as Moore and as Mealy in C. Which one reacts a cycle earlier? Which one is easier to debug, and why?"},{"n":"Active objects","t":"key","q":"An active object is a task plus an event queue plus a state machine, sharing nothing. Explain why this architecture has no mutexes and no data races. What did you give up?"},{"n":"Event memory management","q":"Most frameworks heap-allocate events. Design a zero-allocation scheme with static event pools. What is your policy when the pool runs dry - drop, block, or assert?"},{"n":"State machine testing","q":"Your FSM has 6 states and 5 events. How many transitions must you test? Write a host-side test that drives all of them with no hardware."}]}]},{"id":"cm","num":"2","name":"ARM Cortex-M Architecture","month":"Month 2 | W5-6","gap":1,"desc":"Your single largest gap - absent from both your roadmap and your React app. This is what separates 'I used an MCU' from 'Firmware Engineer'. Needs a Nucleo: your ESP32 is Xtensa or RISC-V, not Cortex-M.","topics":[{"n":"The core","subs":[{"n":"Cortex-M family differences","t":"key","q":"Build a table: M0 / M0+ / M3 / M4 / M7 / M33. Columns: DSP, FPU, cache, MPU, TrustZone, bit-banding. Which one is on your Nucleo?"},{"n":"Thumb-2 ISA","t":"aw","q":"Why does Cortex-M only run Thumb? What does the T bit in EPSR do, and what happens if it gets cleared?"},{"n":"Register file R0-R15","t":"key","q":"Name R13, R14, R15 and their special roles. What actually happens to R14 when you execute a BL instruction?"},{"n":"xPSR: APSR, IPSR, EPSR","q":"Read xPSR in the debugger inside an ISR. What does IPSR tell you? Use it to write a function `bool in_isr(void)`."},{"n":"MSP vs PSP and CONTROL","t":"key","q":"Which stack pointer is active after reset? Which does an RTOS task use, and which does an ISR use? Show the CONTROL bit that switches it."},{"n":"Thread vs Handler mode, privilege","q":"Draw the four combinations of (thread/handler) x (privileged/unprivileged). Which are legal? How does unprivileged code get back to privileged?"},{"n":"AAPCS calling convention","t":"key","q":"Which registers pass arguments? Which hold return values? Which must a callee preserve? Now disassemble one of your own C functions and identify each."},{"n":"Memory map regions","q":"Sketch the Cortex-M map: Code, SRAM, Peripheral, External, PPB. At what address does SRAM start? Where does NVIC live?"},{"n":"Bit-banding","t":"aw","q":"Compute the bit-band alias address for bit 5 of 0x20000000. Which cores have it? Why did M7 drop it?"}]},{"n":"Startup and linking","subs":[{"n":"Reset sequence","t":"key","q":"On reset, the core loads two words from address 0. What are they, in order? Trace it in your own vector table."},{"n":"Startup file walkthrough","t":"key","q":"Open startup_stm32f4xx.s. Find where .data is copied from flash to RAM and where .bss is zeroed. Explain why .data needs copying at all."},{"n":"Linker script: MEMORY and SECTIONS","t":"key","q":"Write a minimal linker script for 512 KB flash and 96 KB RAM. Place .isr_vector first. Explain what happens if you get that order wrong."},{"n":"VMA vs LMA","t":"key","q":"For .data, what is the load address and what is the run address? Why are they different, and which symbol tells startup where to copy from?"},{"n":"Stack and heap placement","q":"Where does _estack point? Show the collision case where the stack grows into the heap. What does the linker do about it (hint: nothing)."},{"n":"Map file analysis","q":"Build your project and open the .map. Find the three largest symbols. Find total flash and RAM used. Explain one thing you did not expect to be there."}]},{"n":"Exceptions and interrupts","subs":[{"n":"Vector table and VTOR","t":"key","q":"Write out the first 16 vector entries in order. Then relocate the table to RAM by writing VTOR - and state which instruction must follow the write."},{"n":"Automatic stacking","t":"key","q":"On exception entry the core pushes 8 registers automatically. Name them in stack order. Why exactly these eight (hint: AAPCS)?"},{"n":"EXC_RETURN","t":"key","q":"You are in a handler and LR = 0xFFFFFFFD. Decode it: which stack was in use, thread or handler mode, FPU state stacked or not?"},{"n":"Tail-chaining and late arrival","q":"Two ISRs are pending back to back. Explain what tail-chaining skips and roughly how many cycles it saves."},{"n":"NVIC enable, pending, priority","t":"key","q":"Enable EXTI0, set priority 5, then trigger it in software via NVIC_ISPR. Do it with raw register writes, then with CMSIS."},{"n":"Priority grouping","t":"key","q":"With 4 priority bits and group setting 5, how many preempt levels and sub levels? Now: which is higher priority, 0x20 or 0x40 - and why is that counter-intuitive?"},{"n":"PRIMASK vs FAULTMASK vs BASEPRI","t":"key","q":"Explain what each masks. You need to block priorities 5-15 but let 0-4 through - which one do you use and with what value? Why does FreeRTOS use exactly this?"},{"n":"SVC instruction","q":"Trigger SVC #3 from thread mode. In the handler, recover the immediate operand 3 from the stacked PC. Why is this the basis of a syscall layer?"},{"n":"PendSV and context switch","t":"key","q":"Why is PendSV set to the lowest priority? Explain the failure if an RTOS switched context directly from SysTick while an ISR was still pending."},{"n":"SysTick","q":"Configure SysTick for a 1 ms tick at 84 MHz. Compute the reload value. Which clock source did you pick and why does the /8 option exist?"}]},{"n":"Fault handling","gap":1,"subs":[{"n":"HFSR - HardFault status","t":"key","q":"Deliberately dereference address 0x00000000. In the debugger, read HFSR. Which bit is set? What does FORCED actually mean?"},{"n":"CFSR = MMFSR + BFSR + UFSR","t":"key","q":"Decode CFSR = 0x00008200. Which sub-register, which bit, which fault? Do it by hand from the reference manual, not a decoder website."},{"n":"MMFAR and BFAR","t":"key","q":"Cause a bus fault at a bad address. Read BFAR. Now check BFARVALID - under what condition is BFAR meaningless?"},{"n":"Imprecise bus fault","t":"key","q":"Your BFSR shows IMPRECISERR and BFAR is garbage. Explain why the core lost the address. Then show where a DSB would have made it precise."},{"n":"UsageFault causes","q":"Enable UsageFault in SHCSR, then trigger UNALIGNED and DIVBYZERO separately (enable them in CCR first). Read UFSR for each."},{"n":"Writing a real HardFault handler","t":"key","q":"Write a naked HardFault_Handler that inspects EXC_RETURN to pick MSP or PSP, then passes the stack frame to a C function. Extract the faulting PC and LR."},{"n":"Postmortem with addr2line","t":"key","q":"Take the faulting PC from above and run `arm-none-eabi-addr2line -e fw.elf <PC>`. Get the exact file and line. This is the whole skill - demonstrate it end to end."},{"n":"Persisting fault context across reset","q":"Store the fault frame in a .noinit RAM section, reset, then print it on next boot. Why .noinit and not .data or .bss?"},{"n":"Reset cause detection","q":"Read RCC_CSR at boot and print which of POR / NRST / IWDG / WWDG / software caused the reset. Then clear the flags - why must you?"}]},{"n":"MPU, FPU, cache","subs":[{"n":"MPU regions and attributes","q":"Configure an MPU region making .text read-only and RAM never-execute. Trigger a violation deliberately and catch the MemManage fault."},{"n":"MPU as a stack guard","t":"key","q":"Place a small no-access MPU region just below a task's stack. Overflow the stack and show it faults instead of silently corrupting the neighbour."},{"n":"FPU enable and lazy stacking","q":"Enable the FPU via CPACR. Then explain lazy stacking: what does the core defer, and what bug appears in an RTOS if you configure it wrong?"},{"n":"Soft-float vs hard-float ABI","q":"Build the same float code with -mfloat-abi=soft and =hard. Compare cycle count using DWT->CYCCNT and compare code size."},{"n":"Cortex-M7 caches","t":"aw","q":"Explain I-cache vs D-cache, and write-through vs write-back. Which is the safer default when you are also using DMA?"},{"n":"DMA cache coherency","t":"key","q":"You DMA a buffer in and read stale data. Explain exactly why. Then state whether you clean or invalidate for RX, and for TX - and why buffers must be 32-byte aligned."}]},{"n":"Synchronization primitives","subs":[{"n":"DMB, DSB, ISB","t":"key","q":"For each: one concrete Cortex-M case where omitting it is a real bug. Start with the barrier required after writing VTOR, and after disabling an interrupt."},{"n":"LDREX / STREX","q":"Implement an atomic increment using LDREX/STREX with a retry loop. What clears the exclusive monitor, and why does a context switch have to?"},{"n":"WFI vs WFE","q":"Explain the difference. Which one wakes on a pending-but-masked interrupt? Write a low-power idle loop using each."},{"n":"DWT cycle counter","t":"key","q":"Enable DWT->CYCCNT and measure the exact cycle cost of your I2C read. Convert to us. This is your profiler - use it before you optimize anything."},{"n":"CMSIS-Core","q":"Find __NOP, __WFI, __disable_irq, __BKPT in the CMSIS headers. Read the inline assembly behind one of them."},{"n":"TrustZone-M","t":"aw","q":"On M33: what are secure and non-secure states? What do SAU and NSC do? Name one product feature this enables."}]},{"n":"Assembly and the C runtime","subs":[{"n":"Read an ARM disassembly","t":"key","q":"Compile a 5-line C function and run objdump -d on it. Point at the prologue, the body, and the epilogue. Which registers were pushed, and why those?"},{"n":"Thumb instruction basics","q":"Recognise PUSH/POP, LDR/STR, B/BL/BX, MOV, CMP and the condition codes. Then hand-trace your own Reset_Handler, instruction by instruction, to the call to main."},{"n":"Inline assembly in GCC","q":"Write an __asm volatile block that disables interrupts (CPSID i). Explain the constraint list and the clobber list. Why does volatile matter here as well?"},{"n":"Naked functions","t":"key","q":"Write a naked HardFault handler using __attribute__((naked)). What does naked remove? Why is that exactly what you need before inspecting the stacked frame?"},{"n":"Pipeline and the PC offset","q":"On Cortex-M, reading the PC gives you the current instruction plus an offset. Explain why, using the pipeline. What does a taken branch cost?"},{"n":"newlib and the C runtime","t":"key","q":"Where does printf actually put characters? Retarget _write (or _putchar) to your UART. Then explain what _sbrk is, and what malloc does when you have not provided one."},{"n":"Semihosting","q":"Enable semihosting and printf through the debugger with no UART wired. Measure how long one printf blocks for. Now explain what happens if you ship it enabled and no debugger is attached."},{"n":"RISC-V - and why you care","t":"key","q":"Your ESP32-C3/C6/H2 is RISC-V; the original ESP32 is Xtensa; your Nucleo is ARM. Name three differences between RISC-V and Cortex-M: register file, ISA philosophy, interrupt controller. How much of your Cortex-M knowledge transfers?"},{"n":"ISA and computer architecture","t":"aw","q":"Define ISA vs microarchitecture. Why is ARM dominant in embedded and RISC-V gaining? What does 'open ISA' actually buy a chip vendor?"}]}]},{"id":"drv","num":"3","name":"Bare-Metal Drivers","month":"Month 2 | W7","desc":"Register-level, zero HAL, written from the reference manual. If you can only do it with CubeMX, you cannot do it.","topics":[{"n":"Reset and clock","subs":[{"n":"HSI, HSE, LSI, LSE","q":"Name all four on your Nucleo and their frequencies. Which survives a Stop-mode sleep? Which one clocks the independent watchdog?"},{"n":"PLL configuration","t":"key","q":"From an 8 MHz HSE, configure the PLL for 84 MHz SYSCLK. Compute M, N, P. Check the VCO stays inside its legal range - state that range."},{"n":"Clock tree and bus prescalers","t":"key","q":"With SYSCLK=84 MHz, set AHB=84, APB1=42, APB2=84. Now: what frequency does a TIM2 counter actually see, and why is it not 42 MHz?"},{"n":"Flash wait states","t":"key","q":"Set SYSCLK to 84 MHz without changing flash latency. Watch it hang or fault. Explain the cause, then find the required wait states in the datasheet table."},{"n":"RCC peripheral clock enable","t":"key","q":"Write to GPIOA->MODER without enabling the GPIOA clock in RCC. Read the register back. Explain what you observed - this is the #1 'my GPIO is dead' answer."},{"n":"Clock Security System","t":"aw","q":"Enable CSS, then physically stop the HSE crystal. What happens? Which interrupt fires and what does the core fall back to?"},{"n":"MCO clock output","q":"Route SYSCLK/4 to the MCO pin and measure it on a scope. You now have physical proof your clock config is correct - why is this worth doing every bring-up?"}]},{"n":"GPIO and EXTI","subs":[{"n":"GPIO modes","q":"Set PA5 to output push-pull, 2 MHz, no pull. Write every register (MODER, OTYPER, OSPEEDR, PUPDR) by hand. Blink it."},{"n":"Push-pull vs open-drain","t":"key","q":"Configure a pin open-drain and try to drive it high. Scope it. Now add a pull-up. Explain why I2C physically requires this mode."},{"n":"Pull-ups, pull-downs, floating","t":"key","q":"Leave an unused input floating and read it 1000 times. Now measure sleep current with it floating vs pulled. Explain the microamps."},{"n":"Output speed and EMI","q":"Toggle a pin at 2 MHz setting vs 100 MHz setting. Scope the rise time and the ringing. State the trade-off you just measured."},{"n":"Alternate function multiplexer","q":"Find in the datasheet which AF number maps PA9 to USART1_TX. Write AFRH by hand. Why is there a mux at all?"},{"n":"BSRR atomic set/reset","t":"key","q":"Toggle a pin using ODR |= and using BSRR. Diff the disassembly. Now show the ISR race that BSRR eliminates."},{"n":"EXTI configuration","q":"Configure PC13 for a falling-edge interrupt: SYSCFG mux, EXTI IMR, FTSR, NVIC enable. Do not forget to clear the pending flag - show what happens if you do."},{"n":"Shared EXTI lines","q":"EXTI9_5 is one handler for five lines. Write the handler that determines which line actually fired."},{"n":"Debouncing","t":"key","q":"Scope a real button press and count the bounces. Then implement two fixes: an RC filter, and a timer-based software debounce. Which belongs in an ISR?"}]},{"n":"Timers","subs":[{"n":"Prescaler and auto-reload","t":"key","q":"From an 84 MHz timer clock, produce a 1 Hz update interrupt. Compute PSC and ARR. Show that more than one answer exists and state which you'd pick."},{"n":"Counter modes","q":"Configure up, down, and center-aligned modes. Scope the resulting PWM in each. Why does motor control prefer center-aligned?"},{"n":"PWM generation","t":"key","q":"Generate 20 kHz PWM at 37% duty on PA5. Compute CCR from ARR. Verify duty on a scope to within 1%."},{"n":"Dead-time and complementary outputs","t":"key","q":"On TIM1, enable CH1 and CH1N with dead-time. Compute the DTG register value for 500 ns. Scope both outputs overlapping - prove they never do."},{"n":"Break input","q":"Wire a fault signal to TIM1_BKIN. Trigger it and show PWM going to a safe state in hardware, with no CPU involvement. Why must this be hardware?"},{"n":"Input capture","t":"key","q":"Measure an unknown signal's frequency and duty using input capture on two channels. What is your resolution, and what sets it?"},{"n":"Encoder mode","q":"Wire a quadrature encoder to TIM2 in encoder mode. Read position with zero CPU cost. What does the hardware do about direction and bounce?"},{"n":"Timer chaining and triggers","q":"Make TIM2 trigger TIM3 as a master/slave pair. Then use TIM2 to trigger ADC conversions at exactly 1 kSPS. Why is this better than a software delay?"}]},{"n":"ADC and DAC","subs":[{"n":"Sampling time selection","t":"key","q":"Your source impedance is 50 k ohm. Look up the required sampling time in the datasheet table. Set the SMPR register. Show the error you get if you pick the shortest."},{"n":"Single, continuous, scan mode","q":"Read one channel once. Then read 3 channels continuously in scan mode. Where does the data go and why do you now need DMA?"},{"n":"Regular vs injected channels","q":"Explain the difference. Give a real case where you need an injected conversion to preempt a regular sequence."},{"n":"Timer-triggered ADC","t":"key","q":"Trigger ADC from TIM2 at exactly 1 kSPS. Why does an FFT need this and not a software-timed loop? Quantify the jitter you removed."},{"n":"ADC + DMA circular","t":"key","q":"Set up 4 channels, scan mode, DMA circular into a 4-word buffer. CPU does zero work. Prove it by toggling a pin in main and scoping the duty."},{"n":"Calibration and Vrefint","q":"Run the ADC self-calibration. Then read Vrefint and use it to compute the actual VDDA. Why does this survive a sagging supply?"},{"n":"Hardware oversampling","q":"Enable 16x hardware oversampling. Measure noise before and after with a fixed input. Compare the improvement to the sqrtN prediction."},{"n":"DAC with DMA","t":"aw","q":"Generate a 1 kHz sine on the DAC using a lookup table and timer-triggered DMA. How many CPU cycles does this cost per sample?"}]},{"n":"DMA","subs":[{"n":"Why DMA exists","t":"key","q":"Read 1000 ADC samples with a CPU loop, then with DMA. Measure CPU free time in both using a scope on a toggled pin."},{"n":"Streams, channels, request mapping","q":"Find in the reference manual which stream/channel serves USART2_RX. Why can't you pick any stream you like?"},{"n":"Circular mode","t":"key","q":"Configure circular DMA and explain what the controller reloads automatically. When does the transfer ever stop?"},{"n":"Half-transfer and transfer-complete","t":"key","q":"Use HT and TC interrupts to process a 512-sample buffer in two halves while DMA fills the other. This is ping-pong - draw the timing."},{"n":"FIFO, burst, and width mismatch","q":"Transfer from a 16-bit peripheral to a 32-bit memory buffer. Configure FIFO and data widths. What breaks if you get the alignment wrong?"},{"n":"Priority and arbitration","t":"aw","q":"Two streams contend for the bus. How is the winner chosen? When would you raise a stream's priority in a real design?"},{"n":"UART RX: DMA + IDLE line","t":"key","q":"Receive variable-length frames with zero CPU: DMA circular plus the USART IDLE interrupt. Compute how many bytes arrived. This is the professional pattern - build it."}]},{"n":"RTC, watchdog, power, flash","subs":[{"n":"RTC and backup domain","q":"Configure the RTC from LSE, set an alarm 30 s ahead, and wake from Standby. Which registers survive Standby and why?"},{"n":"IWDG vs WWDG","t":"key","q":"Explain both. Which survives a system-clock failure, and why? Which one catches code that runs too fast as well as too slow?"},{"n":"Watchdog strategy in an RTOS","t":"key","q":"Five tasks must all be alive. Design a check-in scheme where one task feeds the watchdog only if all five reported. Why is feeding from a timer callback wrong?"},{"n":"Reset source detection","t":"key","q":"Read RCC_CSR at boot, decode the reset cause, log it. Then force a watchdog reset and confirm your log. Why must you clear RMVF afterwards?"},{"n":"Brown-out reset and PVD","q":"Set the BOR level. Then use PVD to get an early warning interrupt before the rail collapses. What would you do in those few milliseconds?"},{"n":"Power modes","t":"key","q":"Build a table for Run / Sleep / Stop / Standby: current draw, wake latency, what RAM survives. Measure at least two of them yourself."},{"n":"Flash program and erase","q":"Unlock flash, erase a sector, write a word, lock it again. Why must you erase before write, and why is the erase granularity so much larger than the write?"},{"n":"Option bytes","t":"aw","q":"Read the option bytes with STM32CubeProgrammer. Find the RDP level and the watchdog hardware/software selection. What is irreversible in here?"},{"n":"Hardware CRC and RNG","q":"Compute a CRC-32 over 1 KB with the hardware unit and with a software loop. Compare cycles with DWT->CYCCNT."}]}]},{"id":"dbg","num":"4","name":"Debug & Instrumentation","month":"Month 2 | W8","gap":1,"desc":"Your roadmap says you debug with serial print. This is the upgrade that changes how fast you can work - and it is visible in interviews within two minutes.","topics":[{"n":"Debug transport","subs":[{"n":"SWD vs JTAG","t":"key","q":"Compare pin count and capability. Name one case where JTAG still wins. Which does your Nucleo's ST-LINK use?"},{"n":"Debug probes","q":"Compare ST-LINK, J-Link, DAPLink on: speed, RTT support, SystemView support, price. Which do you have, and what does it cost you?"},{"n":"OpenOCD","q":"Start OpenOCD with an interface and target config, connect via telnet, and run `reset halt` then `mdw 0x08000000 4`. What are you looking at?"}]},{"n":"GDB","subs":[{"n":"Core GDB commands","t":"key","q":"Set a breakpoint in your UART ISR. When it hits: print all registers, backtrace, examine 16 words of the stack, and print a struct in hex."},{"n":"Hardware vs software breakpoints","t":"key","q":"Set 8 breakpoints in flash. Some fail. Explain the FPB comparator limit. Where do software breakpoints work instead?"},{"n":"Watchpoints","q":"A variable is being corrupted and you don't know by whom. Set a data watchpoint on its address and catch the writer. Do this on a real bug."},{"n":"RTOS thread awareness","q":"Enable FreeRTOS awareness in your debugger. Halt and list all tasks with their states and stack usage. Which task is blocked and on what?"},{"n":".gdbinit automation","q":"Write a .gdbinit that connects, loads, resets, and breaks at main in one command. How much time per day does this save you?"}]},{"n":"Trace and logging","subs":[{"n":"Segger RTT","t":"key","q":"Set up RTT and log from inside an ISR. Measure the cost in cycles with DWT->CYCCNT. Compare against printf-over-UART at 115200. State the ratio."},{"n":"Segger SystemView","t":"key","q":"Instrument FreeRTOS with SystemView. Capture a trace showing task switches and ISR entry. Find one place where a task ran longer than you expected."},{"n":"ITM / SWO","q":"Redirect printf to ITM port 0 over SWO. Set the SWO baud from the core clock. What else can ITM carry besides text?"},{"n":"Core dump / postmortem","t":"key","q":"On HardFault, save the fault frame to .noinit RAM, reset, and dump it over UART on next boot. Now you can debug a fault that happened in the field - explain why that matters."}]},{"n":"Binary analysis","subs":[{"n":"size and map file","t":"key","q":"Run arm-none-eabi-size. Explain what text, data, and bss each mean for flash vs RAM. Then find in the .map the single biggest thing you didn't expect."},{"n":"objdump and disassembly","q":"Disassemble one C function. Identify the prologue, the epilogue, and where AAPCS argument registers are used."},{"n":"nm --size-sort","q":"List your ten largest symbols. Pick one and reduce it. What did you actually cut?"},{"n":"addr2line","t":"key","q":"Given a raw PC value from a fault, get file and line. Practice until it is reflex - this is the single most useful command in firmware debugging."},{"n":"Stack usage analysis","t":"key","q":"Build with -fstack-usage and find your deepest function. Then paint the stack with 0xAA at boot and measure real high-water usage. Compare the two numbers."}]},{"n":"Bench instruments","subs":[{"n":"Oscilloscope fundamentals","t":"key","q":"Your scope is 100 MHz. What is the fastest rise time it can honestly show (BW ~ 0.35/t_r)? Measure a real GPIO edge and state whether you believe the number."},{"n":"Probe compensation and ground lead","t":"key","q":"Capture the same fast edge with the long ground clip, then with a spring tip. Screenshot both. Explain the ringing you just invented."},{"n":"Triggering","q":"Capture a glitch that happens once per minute. Which trigger mode? Now trigger on a specific I2C address using serial decode."},{"n":"Logic analyzer","t":"key","q":"Capture an I2C transaction in PulseView. Decode it. Then measure the actual rise time of SDA and compare against the I2C spec limit for your speed."},{"n":"Sample rate vs signal rate","q":"You capture a 1 MHz SPI clock at 2 MSPS. Why is the decode wrong? State the rule of thumb and re-capture correctly."},{"n":"Multimeter and burden voltage","q":"Measure your board's current with a DMM in series. Now explain burden voltage and why your 3.3 V rail just sagged."},{"n":"Bench PSU current limiting","t":"key","q":"Set a current limit before powering a new board for the first time. What limit would you pick, and what does it save you from?"},{"n":"Current profiling","q":"Capture the current profile of one BLE advertising event: sleep, wake, radio TX, back to sleep. Use a shunt plus scope, or a PPK2. Compute average current."}]},{"n":"Bring-up methodology","subs":[{"n":"Bring-up order","t":"key","q":"Write the checklist you follow on a brand-new board, in order: rails -> reset -> clock -> SWD -> boot -> peripherals. Why does clock come before SWD?"},{"n":"Datasheet vs reference manual vs errata","t":"key","q":"For your MCU, find one item in each document that the other two do not contain. Then find one erratum that would have cost you a day."},{"n":"Renode / QEMU","t":"aw","q":"Run one of your firmware unit tests in Renode with no hardware attached. What can it not simulate, and does that matter for CI?"}]},{"n":"Prototyping and rework","subs":[{"n":"Breadboarding","q":"Build your op-amp circuit on a breadboard. Now scope the rail while switching a load. Explain the noise you see in terms of the breadboard's parasitic inductance and contact resistance."},{"n":"Through-hole soldering","q":"Solder a pin header. Photograph a good joint (shiny, concave fillet) next to a cold joint. What causes a cold joint, and why does it pass continuity but fail later?"},{"n":"SMD soldering by hand","t":"key","q":"Hand-solder an 0603 and an SOIC-8. Then reflow a QFN with hot air. The QFN has a failure you cannot see - name it, and say how you would detect it without an X-ray."},{"n":"Rework and desoldering","q":"Remove a soldered TQFP without lifting a pad. Which tool, what temperature, what is the most common way people destroy the board here?"},{"n":"Flux, wick and tools","q":"What does flux actually do? Why does no-clean still need cleaning sometimes? Fix a solder bridge with wick and report what you learned."},{"n":"Function generator","q":"Inject a 1 kHz sine into the filter you built in Section 0 and sweep the frequency to find the -3 dB point by hand. Compare against your LTspice prediction. Which was wrong?"}]}]},{"id":"proto","num":"5","name":"Wired Protocols","month":"Month 2 | W9-10","desc":"Every protocol here gets bit-banged once, then run on hardware, then verified on a logic analyzer against the datasheet. Knowing the API is not knowing the protocol.","topics":[{"n":"General principles","subs":[{"n":"Single-ended vs differential","t":"key","q":"Why does CAN survive a noisy factory but UART does not? Explain common-mode rejection using a concrete 2 V noise spike."},{"n":"Synchronous vs asynchronous","q":"SPI has a clock; UART does not. What must both ends of a UART agree on beforehand, and what is the tolerance?"},{"n":"Checksum vs CRC","t":"key","q":"A simple sum misses a two-bit error that CRC-16 catches. Construct such a case. What property of CRC makes it stronger?"}]},{"n":"UART","subs":[{"n":"Frame format","q":"Draw an 8N1 frame for the byte 0x41 on a scope timeline. Mark start, LSB-first data, and stop. Now capture it for real and compare."},{"n":"Baud rate and tolerance","t":"key","q":"Your MCU runs 8.2% off its nominal clock. At 115200 baud, will the link work? Derive the tolerance budget for 8N1 and justify the +/-2-3% rule."},{"n":"Oversampling 8x vs 16x","q":"What does the receiver sample at, and where in the bit? What do you gain by dropping to 8x oversampling, and what do you lose?"},{"n":"Framing, parity, overrun, noise errors","t":"key","q":"Deliberately mismatch baud rates and read the error flags. Which one fires? Then cause an overrun by not reading DR fast enough."},{"n":"Flow control RTS/CTS","q":"Your receiver's buffer fills. Show how RTS stops the sender. Why is software XON/XOFF unusable for binary data?"},{"n":"RS-232 vs TTL levels","t":"key","q":"You connected a PC RS-232 port straight to a 3.3 V MCU pin. What voltage did the pin see, and is the MCU alive? Name the correct part."},{"n":"ISR + ring buffer RX","t":"key","q":"Receive 115200 baud continuously with zero loss. Implement RX ISR into a ring buffer, main loop drains it. Prove no loss over 1 M bytes."}]},{"n":"SPI","subs":[{"n":"Signals and roles","q":"Name all four lines. Wire two slaves. Why does each slave need its own CS, and what must a slave do when its CS is high?"},{"n":"CPOL and CPHA - modes 0-3","t":"key","q":"Draw all four modes' waveforms. Your sensor datasheet says 'data sampled on rising edge, clock idles low' - which mode? Set it and verify on the LA."},{"n":"Full-duplex nature","t":"key","q":"You only want to read. Explain why you must still transmit dummy bytes. Show the exchange on the LA."},{"n":"Clock speed and timing margins","q":"Your slave allows 10 MHz. Your traces are 15 cm. Compute round-trip delay and decide whether 10 MHz is safe. What actually fails first?"},{"n":"Bit-bang SPI","t":"key","q":"Implement SPI mode 0 with pure GPIO - no peripheral. Read your sensor. Then capture it on the LA and prove the timing matches the datasheet."},{"n":"SPI + DMA","q":"Stream a 320x240 frame to a TFT over SPI+DMA. Measure CPU load with a toggled pin. What frame rate did you reach?"}]},{"n":"I2C","subs":[{"n":"Open-drain and pull-ups","t":"key","q":"Why must SDA/SCL be open-drain? What happens electrically if two devices drove push-pull and disagreed?"},{"n":"Pull-up sizing","t":"key","q":"Bus capacitance is 200 pF, target 400 kHz. Compute the maximum pull-up resistance from the rise-time limit. Now measure your actual rise time on a scope."},{"n":"START, STOP, repeated START","t":"key","q":"Why does a register read need a repeated START instead of STOP-then-START? What could another master do in that gap?"},{"n":"Addressing and conflicts","q":"Two sensors both answer at 0x68. List three ways to resolve it. Which needs no extra hardware?"},{"n":"ACK and NACK","q":"Capture a transaction to a non-existent address. Where is the NACK on the waveform? How does your driver detect it?"},{"n":"Clock stretching","t":"key","q":"Explain what a slave is doing when it holds SCL low. Then find a master peripheral that doesn't support it - what breaks?"},{"n":"Multi-master arbitration","q":"Two masters start simultaneously. Explain wired-AND arbitration bit by bit. Which one loses, and does it lose data?"},{"n":"Bus recovery","t":"key","q":"A slave is stuck holding SDA low after a reset mid-byte. Write the 9-clock recovery routine. Why exactly nine?"},{"n":"I2C bus scanner","t":"key","q":"Write a scanner from scratch - no library - that lists every responding address 0x08-0x77. Explain what you send to test each."}]},{"n":"CAN","gap":1,"subs":[{"n":"Differential signalling and termination","t":"key","q":"Draw CANH and CANL for dominant and recessive. Why 120 ohm at both ends and not one? Measure the bus with a DMM (power off) - what should you read?"},{"n":"Dominant vs recessive","t":"key","q":"Which bit value is dominant? Explain how that single choice makes wired-AND arbitration possible."},{"n":"Bitwise arbitration","t":"key","q":"Node A sends ID 0x100, node B sends 0x080, simultaneously. Walk bit by bit and show who wins and when B drops out. Why is this non-destructive?"},{"n":"Frame types","q":"Name data, remote, error, and overload frames. Draw a standard data frame's fields in order. Where is the CRC, and what does it cover?"},{"n":"Bit stuffing","q":"After how many identical consecutive bits is a stuff bit inserted? Why does CAN need this, and where in the frame does stuffing stop?"},{"n":"Bit timing and sample point","t":"key","q":"For 500 kbit/s from a 42 MHz clock, compute BRP and the segment values for an 87.5% sample point. Why does the sample point sit so late?"},{"n":"SJW and resynchronization","q":"Two nodes' oscillators drift apart. Explain how SJW lets the receiver re-align. What happens if SJW is too small?"},{"n":"Error counters and bus-off","t":"key","q":"Explain TEC and REC. At what count does a node go error-passive, and at what count bus-off? Force bus-off by shorting the bus, then recover."},{"n":"Filters and masks","q":"Configure a filter accepting only IDs 0x200-0x20F. Write the ID/mask pair. Why do this in hardware and not in software?"}]},{"n":"RS-485 and LIN","subs":[{"n":"RS-485 half-duplex and DE/RE","t":"key","q":"Show the DE turnaround problem: you released DE before the last stop bit finished. What arrives at the other end? Fix it using the UART TC flag."},{"n":"Termination and fail-safe biasing","q":"With no node driving, the bus floats and the receiver outputs garbage. Design the fail-safe bias resistors. What do they guarantee?"},{"n":"Modbus RTU framing","t":"aw","q":"How does Modbus RTU delimit a frame with no start byte? Explain the 3.5-character silence rule and why it's awkward at high baud."},{"n":"LIN bus","t":"aw","q":"Draw a LIN frame: break, sync, PID, data, checksum. Why does LIN exist when CAN already does?"}]},{"n":"USB","subs":[{"n":"Differential D+/D- and speed detection","q":"How does a host know if a device is Low or Full speed before any data flows? Which line gets the pull-up?"},{"n":"Packet types","q":"Name token, data, handshake, SOF. Draw the packet sequence for one successful bulk OUT transfer."},{"n":"Transfer types","t":"key","q":"Match each to a real device: control, bulk, interrupt, isochronous. Which guarantees bandwidth but not delivery? Which is the opposite?"},{"n":"Endpoints and EP0","q":"What is endpoint 0 always used for? How many endpoints does a CDC-ACM device need, and in which directions?"},{"n":"Enumeration sequence","t":"key","q":"List every step from plug-in to driver loaded. Capture a real enumeration in Wireshark with USBPcap and match your list against it."},{"n":"Descriptors","t":"key","q":"Draw the descriptor tree: device -> configuration -> interface -> endpoint. Then dump a real device's descriptors with pyusb and read them."},{"n":"CDC, HID, DFU classes","t":"key","q":"Implement USB CDC on your Nucleo - a virtual COM port with no FTDI chip. Then explain why DFU is the class that matters for your bootloader."}]},{"n":"Ethernet","subs":[{"n":"MAC vs PHY","t":"aw","q":"Which lives inside the MCU and which is external? What crosses the boundary between them?"},{"n":"MII vs RMII","t":"aw","q":"Compare signal count and clock. Why did RMII exist, and what did it cost? Which would you pick on a pin-constrained board?"},{"n":"lwIP integration","t":"aw","q":"Where do DMA descriptor rings sit between the MAC and lwIP? What is zero-copy here, and why do you want it?"}]},{"n":"Other protocols worth recognising","subs":[{"n":"I3C","t":"aw","q":"What does I3C add over I2C - in-band interrupts, dynamic addressing, higher speed. When would you pick it over I2C, and what is the catch today?"},{"n":"I2S and PCM audio","t":"aw","q":"Draw an I2S frame: BCLK, WS (LRCLK), SD. How does WS encode left vs right channel? Where does the bit-clock frequency come from, given sample rate and bit depth?"},{"n":"EtherCAT","t":"aw","q":"Why is EtherCAT faster than a normal Ethernet fieldbus? Explain processing-on-the-fly - the frame is not stored and forwarded. What does that buy a motion controller?"},{"n":"PCIe","t":"aw","q":"Name the difference between a shared bus and a point-to-point link. Why did PCI become PCIe? What is a lane?"},{"n":"MIPI CSI-2 and DSI","t":"aw","q":"Your board needs a camera. What is CSI-2, and why can you not bit-bang it? What does the D-PHY do that a GPIO cannot?"},{"n":"SDIO vs SPI mode","t":"aw","q":"For an SD card, compare SDIO and SPI mode on throughput and pin count. Which does a cheap Arduino shield use, and what did it cost you?"}]}]},{"id":"rtos","num":"6","name":"RTOS - FreeRTOS","month":"Month 3 | W11-12","desc":"One RTOS mastered beats two half-known. Note: your React app has no RTOS domain at all, while your HTML roadmap gives it a full week - this is the biggest gap between your two files.","topics":[{"n":"Concepts","subs":[{"n":"Super-loop vs RTOS","t":"key","q":"Write the same 3-activity system both ways. Now add a hard 5 ms deadline to one activity. Which design meets it, and what did the other one cost you?"},{"n":"Hard vs firm vs soft real-time","t":"key","q":"Classify: an airbag trigger, a video frame, a UI button. For each, state what happens when the deadline is missed."},{"n":"Task states","q":"Draw the state machine: Running, Ready, Blocked, Suspended. Name the API call that causes every transition."},{"n":"TCB and task stack","q":"What lives in a TCB? Where does a task's stack come from with xTaskCreate vs xTaskCreateStatic?"}]},{"n":"Scheduler","subs":[{"n":"Preemptive vs cooperative","q":"Run two equal-priority tasks with time-slicing on, then off. Observe the difference with SystemView. When is cooperative actually safer?"},{"n":"Idle task and hooks","q":"What priority is the idle task, and what does it do? Why must you never block inside the idle hook?"},{"n":"Tick interrupt","q":"At configTICK_RATE_HZ = 1000, what is the cost per tick in cycles? Measure it. What is the accuracy limit of vTaskDelay(1)?"},{"n":"Context switch via PendSV","t":"key","q":"Read FreeRTOS's PendSV_Handler assembly. Which registers does it save manually and why not all of them? Where does PSP get stored?"},{"n":"Why PendSV and not SysTick","t":"key","q":"Explain the failure if the RTOS switched context directly inside SysTick while another ISR was still pending. This is the classic question - answer it in three sentences."},{"n":"FPU context switching","t":"aw","q":"With lazy stacking on, what does the RTOS have to do differently for tasks that use the FPU?"},{"n":"xTaskCreate vs xTaskCreateStatic","q":"Convert a project to fully static allocation. What must you provide, and what did you gain for a safety-critical build?"},{"n":"vTaskDelay vs vTaskDelayUntil","t":"key","q":"A task must run exactly every 10 ms but its work takes a variable 2-4 ms. Show the drift with vTaskDelay over 1000 cycles. Fix it and prove the fix."},{"n":"Priority assignment strategy","q":"You have 5 tasks. State your rule for assigning priorities and defend it. What goes wrong if two unrelated tasks share a priority?"}]},{"n":"Synchronization","subs":[{"n":"Queues and copy semantics","t":"key","q":"Send a 200-byte struct through a queue. How many copies happened? Now send a pointer instead - what new bug did you just create?"},{"n":"Queue blocking and timeouts","q":"A producer is faster than the consumer. Show what happens with portMAX_DELAY vs a 10 ms timeout vs 0. Which is right for a sensor, and why?"},{"n":"xQueueSendFromISR and yield","t":"key","q":"Send from an ISR. Explain what pxHigherPriorityTaskWoken is for, and what latency you add if you forget portYIELD_FROM_ISR."},{"n":"Binary vs counting semaphore","t":"key","q":"Give one correct use of each. Then explain why a binary semaphore is the wrong tool for mutual exclusion."},{"n":"Mutex and recursive mutex","q":"Take the same mutex twice in one task with a plain mutex. What happens? Now with a recursive mutex. When is recursion a design smell?"},{"n":"Priority inversion","t":"key","q":"Build it on hardware: low task holds a mutex, medium task preempts, high task starves. Capture it in SystemView. Measure the actual delay."},{"n":"Priority inheritance","t":"key","q":"Fix the above with a FreeRTOS mutex. Capture again and show the low task's priority being boosted. Explain the mechanism, then name what inheritance still does not solve."},{"n":"Deadlock","t":"key","q":"Create a deadlock with two tasks and two mutexes. Name the four Coffman conditions. Fix it with lock ordering."},{"n":"Critical sections and nesting","q":"How long is too long inside taskENTER_CRITICAL? Measure your longest one with DWT. What did it do to your worst-case interrupt latency?"},{"n":"configMAX_SYSCALL_INTERRUPT_PRIORITY","t":"key","q":"Call xQueueSendFromISR from an ISR with priority above this threshold. Watch configASSERT fire. Explain the BASEPRI boundary and why it exists."},{"n":"Task notifications","t":"key","q":"Replace a binary semaphore with a task notification. Measure both with DWT. State the speed and RAM difference, and the one case where notifications don't work."},{"n":"Event groups","q":"Three sensors must all report before processing starts. Implement with an event group waiting on ALL bits. Now change it to ANY - what use case is that?"},{"n":"Stream and message buffers","t":"aw","q":"When would you pick a stream buffer over a queue? What is the single-writer restriction, and why does it exist?"},{"n":"Software timers","t":"key","q":"Do a periodic sensor read with a software timer instead of a task. Which context does the callback run in? What are you now forbidden from doing inside it?"}]},{"n":"Memory and robustness","subs":[{"n":"heap_1 to heap_5","t":"key","q":"Compare all five in one table: can free, coalesces, fragments, multi-region. Which is the sane default and why? When does heap_1 win?"},{"n":"Fragmentation in an RTOS","q":"Create/delete tasks repeatedly with heap_4 and log the largest free block. Plot it. Now do it with static allocation - plot again."},{"n":"Stack sizing methodology","t":"key","q":"How do you pick a task's stack size? Run it, then read uxTaskGetStackHighWaterMark. State your safety margin and defend the number."},{"n":"Stack overflow hooks","t":"key","q":"Enable checking method 2 and overflow a stack deliberately. Explain how method 1 and method 2 detect it differently, and what each can miss."},{"n":"configASSERT","t":"key","q":"Define configASSERT to break into the debugger. Now trigger three different FreeRTOS assertion failures. What did each teach you?"},{"n":"Runtime stats","q":"Enable vTaskGetRunTimeStats with a high-resolution timer. Print CPU percentage per task. Which task is eating your idle time?"}]},{"n":"Real-time analysis","gap":1,"subs":[{"n":"Interrupt latency vs task latency","t":"key","q":"Measure both on hardware: GPIO edge to ISR first instruction, and GPIO edge to task code running. Explain the gap between the two numbers."},{"n":"WCET estimation","q":"Estimate worst-case execution time for your sensor task. Now measure it 10,000 times with DWT and plot the distribution. Did your estimate hold?"},{"n":"Rate Monotonic Scheduling","t":"key","q":"Tasks: (10 ms period, 3 ms exec), (50 ms, 10 ms), (100 ms, 20 ms). Assign RMS priorities. Compute utilization. Check it against the bound for n=3."},{"n":"Utilization bound","t":"key","q":"State U <= n(2^(1/n) - 1). Compute it for n = 1, 3, and infinity. Your set is at U = 0.72 with n=3 - is it schedulable? What if the test fails?"},{"n":"Tickless idle","q":"Enable tickless idle and measure current before and after. Explain what the RTOS does to the tick, and how it corrects time on wake."},{"n":"SystemView instrumentation","t":"key","q":"Capture a trace with a sensor task, a comms task, and two ISRs. Find the longest ISR. Find one place a task blocked longer than intended."}]}]},{"id":"stor","num":"7","name":"Storage & Filesystems","month":"Month 4 | W13","gap":1,"desc":"Your React app has a bootloader topic but nothing on flash behaviour, wear, or filesystems. This is where OTA and config storage actually live or die.","topics":[{"n":"Flash physics","subs":[{"n":"Erase before write","t":"key","q":"Write 0x00 to a flash word, then try writing 0xFF over it. Read it back. Explain what flash can and cannot do bit-wise without an erase."},{"n":"Erase vs write granularity","t":"key","q":"Your sector is 128 KB but you write 4 bytes. To change one byte, what must you do? Now explain why this alone forces a filesystem to exist."},{"n":"Endurance and retention","q":"Your flash is rated 10,000 cycles. You log once per second to the same sector. How long until it dies? Now design around it."},{"n":"NOR vs NAND","t":"key","q":"Build a table: XIP, random read, erase size, bad blocks, ECC, cost per bit. Which is inside your MCU and which is in an SD card?"},{"n":"External SPI NOR and QSPI","q":"Add a W25Q128 over QSPI and map it into the address space. What can you now do that you couldn't over plain SPI?"}]},{"n":"Non-volatile storage","subs":[{"n":"EEPROM vs flash","q":"Compare byte-writability and endurance. Why do MCUs increasingly ship without real EEPROM?"},{"n":"EEPROM emulation in flash","t":"key","q":"Design a two-sector emulation scheme: write records forward, and page-swap when full. Where is the power-fail hole in your design?"},{"n":"Wear levelling","t":"key","q":"Distinguish dynamic and static wear levelling. Your config sector is rewritten constantly while your firmware sector never is - which one fixes that?"},{"n":"Power-fail safety","t":"key","q":"Cut power mid-write. Design a commit scheme that guarantees the old value survives if the new one didn't complete. Then actually test it by pulling power 20 times."},{"n":"Copy-on-write vs journaling","q":"Explain both. Which does LittleFS use, and what does it buy on an MCU with 20 KB of RAM?"}]},{"n":"Filesystems","subs":[{"n":"LittleFS","t":"key","q":"Mount LittleFS on external flash. Write a file, pull power mid-write, remount. Did it survive? Explain the design property that made that true."},{"n":"LittleFS RAM cost","q":"Compute the RAM footprint from its config: read/prog/cache/lookahead buffers. Is it affordable on your part?"},{"n":"FatFs","q":"Mount a FAT filesystem on an SD card. Why do you use FAT here and not LittleFS? What does FAT do badly on raw flash?"},{"n":"ESP-IDF NVS","q":"Store a Wi-Fi credential in NVS. Explain the key-value model, the partition table entry, and what NVS encryption protects against."},{"n":"SD card: SDIO vs SPI mode","t":"aw","q":"Compare throughput and pin count. Which mode does your driver use, and what did you give up?"}]},{"n":"Application patterns","subs":[{"n":"Config storage pattern","t":"key","q":"Design a config record: magic, version, payload, CRC, dual slot A/B. Show the read path that picks a good slot, and the write path that never destroys both."},{"n":"Config schema versioning","q":"Your v2 firmware finds a v1 config. Write the migration path. What if it finds v3 (a downgrade)?"},{"n":"Logging architecture","t":"key","q":"Design a logger: RAM ring buffer, deferred flush to flash, severity levels. Where do you flush from, and what happens if you flush from an ISR?"},{"n":"Log transport cost","q":"Compare printf-over-UART, RTT, and flash logging: cycles per message, and what survives a reset. Measure at least two."},{"n":"Assert strategy in production","t":"key","q":"Should assert stay enabled in a shipped product? Argue both sides, then state what your assert handler does in the field."}]}]},{"id":"boot","num":"8","name":"Bootloader & OTA","month":"Month 4 | W14-15","gap":1,"desc":"Your React app has a bootloader topic. OTA is entirely absent from both files - and OTA is what makes an IoT product a product.","topics":[{"n":"Bootloader","subs":[{"n":"Boot flow and BOOT pins","q":"Trace what happens from power-on to your main(): BOOT pin sampling, ST system bootloader in ROM, option bytes. Force entry into the ROM bootloader."},{"n":"Memory partitioning","t":"key","q":"Lay out 512 KB flash: bootloader, slot A, slot B, metadata. Give addresses and sizes. Why must the bootloader sit at 0x08000000?"},{"n":"VTOR relocation","t":"key","q":"Your app's vector table is at 0x08008000. What must be written to VTOR, and what instruction must follow the write? Show what happens if you skip it."},{"n":"Jump to application","t":"key","q":"Write the jump: deinit peripherals, disable IRQs, set MSP from app vector[0], set VTOR, then branch to app vector[1]. Reorder two steps and show the HardFault."},{"n":"Image header design","t":"key","q":"Define your header struct: magic, version, length, entry, CRC-32, signature. Where does it sit relative to the vector table, and why does that matter?"},{"n":"CRC-32 validation","t":"key","q":"Validate an image before jumping. Which bytes does the CRC cover - including or excluding the header's own CRC field? Show your answer working."},{"n":"Serial bootloader protocol","t":"key","q":"Design a UART update protocol: handshake, chunk, ACK, retry, finalize. Then implement it. What is your recovery if a chunk is lost?"},{"n":"Recovery mode and factory image","q":"Both slots are corrupt. What now? Design the recovery path and state what makes the board unbrickable."}]},{"n":"OTA","gap":1,"subs":[{"n":"A/B partitions vs overwrite","t":"key","q":"Compare dual-bank A/B against swap/overwrite: flash cost, rollback ability, complexity. Which does your 512 KB part actually allow?"},{"n":"Rollback mechanism","t":"key","q":"New firmware boots, then hangs. Design the confirm/mark-good scheme so the bootloader reverts on next boot. Who sets the good flag, and how late?"},{"n":"Watchdog-gated confirmation","t":"key","q":"Why must the mark-good flag be set only after the app proves itself, not at startup? Design the proof criteria for your own node."},{"n":"Anti-rollback counter","t":"key","q":"An attacker replays your old signed firmware that had a known bug. The signature is still valid. Explain the attack and design the monotonic counter that stops it."},{"n":"Power-loss atomicity","t":"key","q":"Pull power at 10 random points during an OTA. The board must always boot something. Run this test 20 times and report the results."},{"n":"ESP32 OTA","t":"have","q":"Implement OTA on ESP32: partition table, ota_data, esp_ota_begin/write/end/set_boot_partition, then mark valid. What happens if you never mark it valid?"},{"n":"MCUboot","q":"Sign an image with imgtool and boot it under MCUboot. Compare swap vs overwrite mode. What does the scratch partition cost you?"},{"n":"Delta updates","t":"aw","q":"Your firmware is 400 KB and you're on NB-IoT. How much does a full update cost in airtime and battery? What does a delta buy, and what does it complicate?"},{"n":"Firmware versioning","t":"key","q":"Embed git hash and build timestamp into the binary and print them at boot. Now a field unit misbehaves - show how you identify the exact commit."},{"n":"Staged rollout","t":"aw","q":"You have 10,000 devices. Design the rollout: canary, percentage ramp, halt criteria. What metric triggers a halt?"}]}]},{"id":"sec","num":"9","name":"Security","month":"Month 4 | W16","gap":1,"desc":"Zero coverage in either of your files. For IoT roles this is now disqualifying - an unauthenticated OTA is a remote code execution vulnerability you shipped on purpose.","topics":[{"n":"Crypto - usage, never implementation","subs":[{"n":"Threat modelling","t":"key","q":"STRIDE your own sensor node. List assets, attack surfaces, and the single highest-risk entry point. Be honest about the debug header."},{"n":"AES modes","t":"key","q":"Encrypt the same 32-byte plaintext with ECB and with CBC. Look at the ciphertext. Explain the ECB penguin. Then state why GCM beats CBC."},{"n":"AEAD and why GCM","t":"key","q":"What does 'authenticated encryption' add over plain encryption? Show an attack on unauthenticated CTR mode where you flip a plaintext bit without the key."},{"n":"IV / nonce reuse","t":"key","q":"Reuse a nonce twice with AES-GCM. Explain exactly what an attacker recovers. Why is this catastrophic and not just weak?"},{"n":"SHA-256 and hash properties","q":"Define preimage, second preimage, and collision resistance. Which one did MD5 lose first? Why is SHA-1 also retired?"},{"n":"HMAC vs bare hash","t":"key","q":"You append SHA256(message) to a message. Show how an attacker forges a new valid message. Now do it with HMAC - show why it fails."},{"n":"RSA vs ECC on an MCU","q":"Compare key size, signature size, and verify time for RSA-2048 vs ECDSA-P256 at the same security level. Measure verify time with mbedTLS on your board."},{"n":"ECDSA and ECDH","t":"key","q":"Which one signs and which one agrees a shared key? For secure boot, which do you need, and does the private key ever touch the device?"},{"n":"TRNG vs PRNG","t":"key","q":"Seed a PRNG from an uninitialized RAM value. Explain why this is a catastrophic mistake. Then use the hardware RNG and state what entropy actually means."},{"n":"Constant-time comparison","t":"key","q":"Compare an HMAC tag with memcmp. Explain the timing attack byte by byte. Write the constant-time version."}]},{"n":"Secure boot and signing","subs":[{"n":"Root of trust","t":"key","q":"Define it. In your design, what is immutable, and what makes it immutable? What happens if that one thing is compromised?"},{"n":"Public key storage","q":"Where does the verification public key live so an attacker can't swap it? Compare OTP, eFuse, and write-protected flash."},{"n":"Signature verification chain","t":"key","q":"Draw the chain: ROM -> bootloader -> app -> OTA payload. Each link verifies the next. Where does the chain start, and what breaks it?"},{"n":"Code signing in CI","t":"key","q":"Your private key must never be on a developer laptop. Design the CI signing flow. Where does the key live and who can trigger a signed build?"},{"n":"Flash encryption","q":"Explain what flash encryption protects against and what it does not. Does it stop someone with a debugger attached?"},{"n":"ESP32 secure boot v2","t":"aw","q":"Enable secure boot v2 plus flash encryption on ESP32. Which eFuse burns are irreversible? What did you just permanently lose?"}]},{"n":"Transport security","subs":[{"n":"TLS handshake","t":"key","q":"Walk the TLS 1.2 handshake step by step. Where is the server authenticated? Where is the session key agreed, and does it ever cross the wire?"},{"n":"PSK vs certificate","q":"Compare on: provisioning cost, revocation, RAM, flash. When is PSK acceptable for a fleet, and when is it a liability?"},{"n":"X.509 certificates","t":"key","q":"Dump a real cert with `openssl x509 -text`. Find subject, issuer, SAN, validity, and key usage. Which field does hostname verification actually check?"},{"n":"Certificate chain validation","t":"key","q":"Your device trusts a root CA. Walk the validation of a 3-cert chain. Name three checks that must all pass. Which one do embedded devices most often skip?"},{"n":"Mutual TLS","t":"key","q":"Why does IoT need the client to present a cert too? What does server-only TLS leave open? Set up mTLS against Mosquitto."},{"n":"DTLS","q":"Why can't you run TLS over UDP unchanged? Name two things DTLS adds. Where does CoAP use it?"},{"n":"MQTT over TLS","t":"key","q":"Connect to Mosquitto on 8883 with a client certificate. Capture the handshake in Wireshark. Then disable cert validation and show what an attacker can now do."},{"n":"mbedTLS footprint","q":"Trim mbedTLS config to just what you need for MQTT+TLS. Report flash and RAM before and after. What did you remove?"},{"n":"Certificate provisioning","t":"key","q":"10,000 devices each need a unique cert. Design the flow: key generation on-device or off? CSR? Where does injection happen on the production line?"}]},{"n":"Device hardening","subs":[{"n":"Key storage options","q":"Compare OTP, secure element (ATECC608), TPM. For each: can the key ever leave? What does an attacker with the board in hand get?"},{"n":"Secure element vs software crypto","q":"Your MCU has no crypto accelerator. Compare ATECC608 against mbedTLS in software: cost, speed, key protection, BOM complexity."},{"n":"STM32 RDP levels","t":"key","q":"Explain RDP Level 0, 1, and 2. Which is irreversible? Set Level 1 and try to read flash over SWD. Then explain why almost nobody dares set Level 2."},{"n":"ESP32 eFuse JTAG disable","q":"Disable JTAG via eFuse. What else should you disable at the same time? What did you just lose forever in the field?"},{"n":"Write protection and PCROP","t":"aw","q":"What does PCROP protect that plain write-protection does not? Give the use case."},{"n":"TrustZone-M and PSA","t":"aw","q":"On an M33, what would you put in the secure world? Name the PSA Certified levels and what each requires."},{"n":"Side channels: timing","q":"Beyond memcmp, name one more timing leak in a naive crypto implementation. Why does this argue for using a library, not writing your own?"},{"n":"Fault injection and glitching","t":"aw","q":"An attacker glitches VCC during your signature check to skip the branch. How would you make the check glitch-resistant?"},{"n":"Common IoT vulnerabilities","t":"key","q":"Audit your own capstone against: hardcoded credentials, no cert validation, unauthenticated OTA, open debug port, buffer overflow. How many did you have?"},{"n":"OWASP IoT Top 10","q":"Read it. Map each of the 10 to a specific decision in your capstone. Which three are you still violating?"},{"n":"Standards landscape","t":"aw","q":"Name what IEC 62443, ETSI EN 303 645, and the EU Cyber Resilience Act each govern. Which would apply to a smart meter sold in Europe?"}]}]},{"id":"iot","num":"10","name":"Wireless & IoT","month":"Month 5 | W17-18","desc":"Your strongest existing base - Wi-SUN and oneM2M from IIITH are real credentials. This extends them into BLE depth, TLS, and the protocols you're missing.","topics":[{"n":"RF basics","subs":[{"n":"Bands and regulation","q":"Name the band your Wi-SUN deployment used in India, and the 2.4 GHz ISM range. What duty-cycle or power limit applied to you?"},{"n":"Link budget","t":"key","q":"TX power +8 dBm, RX sensitivity -95 dBm, antenna gains 0 dBi. Compute the allowed path loss and the resulting free-space range at 868 MHz. Then explain why real range is far less."},{"n":"RSSI and LQI","q":"Log RSSI vs distance for your node. Why is RSSI a poor predictor of link quality on its own? What does LQI add?"},{"n":"Antenna basics and keepout","q":"Compare chip, PCB trace, and whip antennas. Why does the keepout region exist, and what happens to range if you ignore it?"}]},{"n":"BLE","gap":1,"subs":[{"n":"Stack layers","t":"key","q":"Draw PHY -> LL -> HCI -> L2CAP -> ATT -> GATT -> GAP. Which layer does your application code talk to? Where does the SM sit?"},{"n":"Advertising","t":"key","q":"Set up an advertiser. Capture packets with nRF Connect. Why exactly three advertising channels, and why 37/38/39?"},{"n":"Advertising interval vs discovery time","q":"Advertise at 100 ms vs 1000 ms. Measure average current and time-to-discover for each. State the trade-off in numbers."},{"n":"Connection parameters","t":"key","q":"Explain connection interval, slave latency, and supervision timeout. Pick values for a sensor that reports once a minute but must respond within 2 s. Justify."},{"n":"GATT: services and characteristics","t":"key","q":"Build a custom GATT service with one notify characteristic. Explain the role of the CCCD - what happens if the client never writes it?"},{"n":"Notify vs indicate vs read vs write","t":"key","q":"Compare all four on: who initiates, is it acknowledged, throughput. Your sensor pushes 20 Hz data - which do you pick and why?"},{"n":"MTU negotiation and DLE","q":"Default ATT MTU is 23. Negotiate it up. Measure throughput before and after. What is the other lever (hint: link layer, not ATT)?"},{"n":"PHY: 1M, 2M, Coded","q":"Compare throughput, range, and current for each. Which would you use for an OTA over BLE, and which for a long-range sensor?"},{"n":"GAP roles","q":"Distinguish peripheral, central, broadcaster, observer. Which is your sensor and which is the phone? Which combination needs no connection at all?"},{"n":"Pairing and bonding","t":"key","q":"Explain the difference. Then compare JustWorks, Passkey, and Numeric Comparison - which resist MITM and which do not?"},{"n":"LE Secure Connections","q":"What does LESC add over legacy pairing? Which key exchange does it use? Why does that stop a passive eavesdropper?"},{"n":"Privacy and RPA","t":"aw","q":"A static BLE address lets anyone track your device. Explain resolvable private addresses and who can still resolve them."}]},{"n":"Wi-Fi","subs":[{"n":"STA, AP, SoftAP","t":"have","q":"Run your ESP32 as STA and as SoftAP. What can it do in each? Can it do both at once, and what does that cost?"},{"n":"WPA2 vs WPA3","t":"aw","q":"What attack does WPA3's SAE fix that WPA2's 4-way handshake allowed? Does your ESP32 variant support it?"},{"n":"Provisioning methods","t":"key","q":"Compare SoftAP, BLE provisioning, and SmartConfig on: user friction, security, reliability. Implement one and defend the choice."},{"n":"Power save and DTIM","t":"key","q":"Measure ESP32 current with Wi-Fi power save off, then on with DTIM=3. Report both numbers. What latency did you trade away?"}]},{"n":"Application protocols","subs":[{"n":"MQTT pub/sub and topics","t":"have","q":"Design the topic hierarchy for 1000 nodes x 5 sensors. Show a wildcard subscription that gets all temperature readings. Why is `#` dangerous in production?"},{"n":"QoS 0, 1, 2","t":"key","q":"Draw the packet exchange for each. Which can duplicate? Which can lose? Then pick a QoS for a battery sensor and defend the airtime cost."},{"n":"Retained messages","q":"A dashboard subscribes and sees nothing for 60 s. Fix it with retain. Now explain the stale-data hazard you just introduced."},{"n":"Last Will and Testament","t":"key","q":"Your node loses power. How does the server find out? Configure LWT and prove it by yanking the cable. How long did it take, and what controls that?"},{"n":"Keep-alive and session state","q":"Set keep-alive to 60 s. When does the broker declare you dead? Then compare clean session vs persistent - what does the broker hold for you?"},{"n":"MQTT 5 features","t":"aw","q":"Name two MQTT 5 additions that matter for constrained devices. What is a shared subscription for?"},{"n":"CoAP","gap":1,"t":"key","q":"Compare CoAP to MQTT: transport, model, overhead. Then explain CON vs NON and when a battery node wants NON."},{"n":"CoAP Observe and block-wise","q":"Explain Observe - how is it different from MQTT subscribe? Then explain why block-wise transfer exists over UDP."},{"n":"LwM2M","gap":1,"t":"aw","q":"Describe the object/resource model and the bootstrap step. What does LwM2M give you that raw CoAP does not?"},{"n":"oneM2M","t":"have","q":"You already deployed this. Explain CSE vs AE, and the <cnt>/<cin> resource tree, to an interviewer in 90 seconds. Then say where it fits versus MQTT."}]},{"n":"Network stacks","subs":[{"n":"Wi-SUN FAN","t":"have","q":"Map your IIITH deployment to the stack: PHY (802.15.4g), 6LoWPAN, RPL, UDP. Which layer handled the mesh routing?"},{"n":"6LoWPAN","q":"An IPv6 header is 40 bytes; an 802.15.4 frame carries 127. Explain header compression and fragmentation. What is the practical MTU you had?"},{"n":"RPL routing","t":"aw","q":"Explain the DODAG. How does a node pick its parent? What happens when the parent disappears?"},{"n":"LoRaWAN fundamentals","gap":1,"t":"key","q":"Explain spreading factor: how do SF7 and SF12 differ in airtime, range, and energy for the same payload? Compute airtime for 20 bytes at both."},{"n":"LoRaWAN classes A, B, C","q":"Compare downlink latency and current draw for each. Which can your battery node afford, and what does that mean for remote config?"},{"n":"OTAA vs ABP","t":"key","q":"Compare on security and provisioning. Which keys exist in each? Why is ABP discouraged, and what does the frame counter have to do with it?"},{"n":"Duty cycle and ADR","q":"In EU868 with 1% duty cycle at SF12: how many 20-byte messages per hour can you legally send? Then explain what ADR is trying to fix."},{"n":"NB-IoT vs LTE-M","gap":1,"t":"key","q":"Compare bandwidth, latency, mobility, power. Which suits a static meter, which a moving asset tracker? What does each cost per device per year?"},{"n":"AT command modem drivers","q":"Write a driver that sends AT commands and parses URCs asynchronously. What makes URC handling harder than simple request/response?"},{"n":"PSM and eDRX","t":"key","q":"Explain both. Your device must be reachable within 1 hour and last 10 years on a battery. Which do you configure, and to what value?"},{"n":"Thread and Matter","gap":1,"t":"aw","q":"What does Thread provide that BLE does not? Then: what problem is Matter actually solving, and what is a fabric?"},{"n":"TCP vs UDP for IoT","q":"Your node sends 20 bytes every 10 minutes over cellular. Compute the total bytes for TCP (with handshake) vs UDP. Now justify CoAP/UDP."},{"n":"Serialization: JSON vs CBOR vs Protobuf","t":"key","q":"Encode your actual sensor payload in all three. Report the byte count for each. Multiply by messages-per-year and by your cellular data cost."}]},{"n":"Cloud and fleet","subs":[{"n":"AWS IoT device shadow","gap":1,"t":"key","q":"Explain desired vs reported state. Your device is asleep when config changes - trace what happens on wake. Why does this beat a raw MQTT command?"},{"n":"Azure device twin","gap":1,"t":"aw","q":"Compare to AWS shadow. What are desired properties, reported properties, and tags used for?"},{"n":"Self-hosted stack","t":"key","q":"Stand up Mosquitto + InfluxDB + Grafana. Get your node's data on a dashboard. What do you lose versus a managed cloud, and what do you gain?"},{"n":"Device provisioning at scale","q":"10,000 devices, zero manual steps. Design onboarding: identity, first connect, cert issuance. What is JITP?"},{"n":"Time sync","q":"Your log timestamps drift. Add SNTP. What accuracy did you get, and what does it cost in wake time? When would you need PTP instead?"},{"n":"Edge gateway and store-and-forward","t":"key","q":"The uplink drops for 6 hours. Your gateway must not lose data. Size the buffer, pick an eviction policy, and design the catch-up so you don't flood the broker on reconnect."},{"n":"Reconnect with backoff and jitter","t":"key","q":"1000 devices lose the broker simultaneously and all retry at 5 s. Describe the thundering herd. Now add exponential backoff plus jitter and show the difference."}]}]},{"id":"pwr","num":"11","name":"Power & Low-Power","month":"Month 5 | W18","gap":1,"desc":"Absent from both files. This is what turns 'I built an IoT node' into 'my node runs 4.2 years on a CR2032, and here are the measurements'.","topics":[{"n":"Sleep modes","subs":[{"n":"STM32 power modes","t":"key","q":"Build a table for Run / Sleep / Stop / Standby: current, wake latency, RAM retained, peripherals alive. Measure at least three yourself."},{"n":"ESP32 sleep modes","t":"have","q":"Compare modem sleep, light sleep, deep sleep. What survives in RTC memory? Measure deep-sleep current on your own board."},{"n":"Wake sources","q":"Wake from Standby on an RTC alarm, and separately on a GPIO edge. Which pins can do this, and why only those?"},{"n":"WFI vs WFE and SLEEPDEEP","q":"Write an idle loop with each. Which wakes on a pending-but-masked interrupt? What does SLEEPONEXIT change about ISR return?"},{"n":"Tickless idle","t":"key","q":"Enable it in FreeRTOS. Measure current before and after over one minute. How does the kernel fix up the tick count on wake?"}]},{"n":"Leakage and gating","subs":[{"n":"Peripheral clock gating","q":"Disable every unused peripheral clock and re-measure sleep current. How many uA did you find, and which peripheral was the worst?"},{"n":"Floating GPIO leakage","t":"key","q":"Measure sleep current with an unused pin floating, then pulled, then in analog mode. Report all three. Explain the silent 100 uA."},{"n":"External circuit leakage","q":"Your LED divider draws current in sleep. Find every always-on path on the board and total them. Which one surprised you?"}]},{"n":"Measurement","subs":[{"n":"Measuring uA correctly","t":"key","q":"Your DMM reads 0 uA in deep sleep, but the board still drains. Explain burden voltage and DMM bandwidth. Then measure again with a shunt and scope."},{"n":"Dynamic current profile","t":"key","q":"Capture one full wake-work-sleep cycle on a scope. Mark each phase's current and duration. Integrate to get charge per cycle in uC."},{"n":"Power Profiler Kit","t":"aw","q":"Use a PPK2 (or shunt+scope) to capture a BLE advertising event. What is peak TX current, and for how long?"}]},{"n":"Energy budget","subs":[{"n":"Average current formula","t":"key","q":"Active 20 mA for 50 ms, sleep 3 uA, period 60 s. Compute I_avg. Which term dominates, and what does that tell you to optimize first?"},{"n":"Battery life calculation","t":"key","q":"CR2032 is 220 mAh. With your I_avg above, compute lifetime. Now derate for self-discharge, temperature, and the 2.0 V cutoff. State the honest number."},{"n":"Duty cycling","q":"Halve your reporting interval. Recompute battery life. Is it double? Explain why not."},{"n":"CR2032 pulse current limit","t":"key","q":"A CR2032 has ~10 ohm internal resistance. Your BLE TX pulls 15 mA. Compute the voltage sag. Does the MCU brown out? Design the fix."},{"n":"Chemistry comparison","q":"Compare Li-ion, LiFePO4, alkaline, CR2032 on: nominal V, energy density, self-discharge, temperature range, cost. Pick one for an outdoor node in Hyderabad and defend it."},{"n":"Regulator quiescent current","t":"key","q":"Your MCU sleeps at 3 uA but the board draws 60 uA. Read the LDO's I_q. Explain why regulator choice dominates a sleeping design."},{"n":"Radio energy dominance","t":"key","q":"Compute energy per BLE advertising event vs energy for 1 s of MCU compute. Which is bigger, and by how much? What does that tell you about where to optimize?"},{"n":"Li-ion CC/CV and protection","t":"have","q":"You used TP4056. Draw CC/CV with the termination point. What sets charge current? What happens charging below 0 degC, and does your circuit prevent it?"}]}]},{"id":"test","num":"12","name":"Test, CI & Quality","month":"Month 5 | W19","gap":1,"desc":"Absent from both files. This is the clearest 'professional engineer versus student' signal in an interview - and it costs one week.","topics":[{"n":"Testing strategy","subs":[{"n":"Test pyramid for firmware","t":"key","q":"Draw it: host unit -> host integration with mocks -> on-target -> HIL. What fraction of your logic can be tested with no board attached? Be specific."},{"n":"Design for testability","t":"key","q":"Your sensor logic calls HAL_I2C_Read directly, so it can't be tested off-target. Introduce the HAL seam. Show the before and after headers."},{"n":"Dependency injection in C","q":"Pass a struct of function pointers instead of calling hardware directly. Show the production wiring and the test wiring side by side."},{"n":"Test doubles","q":"Define stub, mock, fake, spy. For your I2C sensor: which do you use to verify the right register was written, and which to return canned data?"}]},{"n":"Tooling","subs":[{"n":"Unity","t":"key","q":"Write 10 tests for your ring buffer: empty, full, wrap, single-element, overflow. Run on host. How long does the whole suite take?"},{"n":"CMock","t":"key","q":"Auto-generate a mock from your HAL header. Write a test that asserts your driver wrote exactly 0x6B then 0x00 to the sensor. Now break the driver and watch it fail."},{"n":"Ceedling","q":"Wire Unity + CMock + build into one `ceedling test:all`. What does the project.yml actually configure?"},{"n":"Host-based testing","t":"key","q":"Compile your protocol parser for x86 and fuzz it with random bytes for 10 seconds. Did it crash? This is why the seam exists."},{"n":"Coverage: gcov and lcov","t":"key","q":"Generate an HTML coverage report for your driver. What is your branch coverage? Find one untested branch and explain why it was missed."},{"n":"Renode in CI","t":"aw","q":"Run your firmware in Renode and assert on UART output. What can't it simulate, and does that matter for a merge gate?"},{"n":"HIL testing","t":"aw","q":"Design a HIL rig for your CAN node: what hardware injects faults, and what asserts pass/fail? What would it cost?"}]},{"n":"Static analysis","subs":[{"n":"Compiler warnings as errors","t":"key","q":"Turn on -Wall -Wextra -Werror on your existing code. How many errors? Fix them all. Which one was a real bug?"},{"n":"cppcheck and clang-tidy","q":"Run both on your repo. Report the top 5 findings each. Which tool found something the compiler didn't?"},{"n":"MISRA C:2012","t":"aw","q":"Run cppcheck --addon=misra. Pick three violations: fix one, and write a deviation record for another. What makes a deviation legitimate?"},{"n":"Sanitizers on host","q":"Build your host tests with -fsanitize=address,undefined. Run them. Did ASan or UBSan catch anything your tests passed?"}]},{"n":"Build and CI","subs":[{"n":"CMake cross-compilation","t":"key","q":"Write a toolchain file for arm-none-eabi. Explain CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME=Generic and why try_compile fails without it."},{"n":"GitHub Actions for firmware","t":"key","q":"Build a workflow: compile, unit test on host, cppcheck, upload the .elf. Make it fail on a real bug and watch the red X."},{"n":"Firmware size regression gate","t":"key","q":"Add a CI step that fails if flash usage grows more than 2%. Why is this worth having in a product?"},{"n":"Embedding git hash into the binary","t":"key","q":"Inject the commit hash and build time at compile time and print at boot. Now a field unit misbehaves - demonstrate the workflow to find the exact source."},{"n":"Size analysis","q":"Run arm-none-eabi-size and read the .map. Find your top 3 flash consumers. Cut one by 20%. What did you actually change?"}]},{"n":"Process","subs":[{"n":"Git branching and releases","q":"Pick trunk-based or gitflow for a 3-person firmware team and defend it. How do you tag a release so you can rebuild it in two years?"},{"n":"Submodules vs subtrees","q":"You vendor FreeRTOS and mbedTLS. Compare submodule and subtree on: pinning, contributor friction, CI. Which did you pick?"},{"n":"Code review discipline","q":"Write the firmware-specific checklist you'd apply to every PR. Include at least three items a web reviewer would never think of."},{"n":"Doxygen and architecture docs","t":"key","q":"Document your driver API with Doxygen. Then draw the system architecture in Mermaid. Would a new engineer be productive from these alone?"},{"n":"Architecture Decision Records","q":"Write an ADR for 'FreeRTOS over Zephyr'. Include context, options, decision, consequences. What does the consequences section force you to admit?"},{"n":"Safety standards","t":"aw","q":"Match each to its industry: ISO 26262, IEC 61508, IEC 62304, DO-178C. What does ASIL D demand that ASIL A does not?"},{"n":"AUTOSAR","t":"aw","q":"What problem does AUTOSAR Classic solve for an OEM with 100 ECUs from 30 suppliers? Why would you care if you interview in automotive?"}]}]},{"id":"math","num":"13","name":"Math & DSP","month":"Woven M1-M5","desc":"Deliberately trimmed to what firmware actually uses. Laplace/Z depth, control theory depth, quantum mechanics and solid-state physics are cut - they are in your React app and they serve none of your three targets.","topics":[{"n":"Number representation","subs":[{"n":"Fixed-point Q15 and Q31","t":"key","q":"Represent 0.75 in Q15 as a hex value. Multiply two Q15 numbers - what intermediate type, and how do you rescale? Where does saturation belong?"},{"n":"Saturation and overflow","q":"Add two Q15 values that overflow. Show wraparound producing a sign flip in an audio sample. Now write the saturating version."},{"n":"IEEE 754 and FPU cost","t":"key","q":"Measure the cycle cost of a float multiply on M4 with the FPU on, then with -mfloat-abi=soft. Report both. When is fixed-point still the right call?"}]},{"n":"Sampling and spectra","subs":[{"n":"Sampling theorem and aliasing","t":"key","q":"Sample a 3 kHz sine at 5 kSPS in Python. Plot the result. What frequency appears, and why?"},{"n":"Anti-alias filter design","t":"key","q":"You sample at 1 kSPS and have real content at 700 Hz. Specify the filter: cutoff, order, attenuation needed at f_s/2. Then build it in LTspice."},{"n":"FFT and bin resolution","t":"key","q":"1024 samples at 8 kSPS. Compute bin width. Which bin holds a 1 kHz tone? Run it on your real ESP32 sensor data and identify the noise sources."},{"n":"Windowing and leakage","q":"FFT a sine that is not an integer number of bins. Observe the smearing. Apply Hann and compare. What did you trade for less leakage?"}]},{"n":"Filtering","subs":[{"n":"FIR vs IIR","t":"key","q":"Compare on: stability, phase linearity, MIPS, memory. Your MCU has no FPU and 20 KB RAM - which do you pick for a 50 Hz notch?"},{"n":"Moving average and EMA","t":"key","q":"Implement both on live sensor data. The EMA is a one-line IIR - write it. Compare step response and memory cost."},{"n":"Median filter","q":"Your sensor has salt-and-pepper spikes. Show why a moving average fails and a median filter works. What does the median cost you in latency?"},{"n":"CMSIS-DSP","q":"Run a CMSIS-DSP Q15 FIR on your board. Compare cycles against your own C loop with DWT->CYCCNT. What is the speedup?"}]},{"n":"Sensors and noise","subs":[{"n":"SNR and noise sources","q":"Define SNR. Name thermal (Johnson-Nyquist), 1/f, and quantization noise. For your sensor chain, which one dominates?"},{"n":"Averaging and the sqrtN rule","t":"key","q":"Average 16 samples. Predict the SNR improvement, then measure it on real data. Under what condition does the sqrtN rule fail?"},{"n":"Calibration: offset and gain","t":"key","q":"Two-point calibrate a temperature sensor. Compute offset and gain by hand, then by least squares. Where do you store the coefficients?"},{"n":"Lookup table with interpolation","q":"Linearize a thermistor with a LUT plus linear interpolation. Size the table for 0.5 degC accuracy. Compare against computing the Steinhart-Hart equation."},{"n":"Complementary filter","t":"aw","q":"Fuse accelerometer and gyro into one angle. Write the one-line complementary filter and explain what each sensor contributes."},{"n":"Kalman filter","t":"aw","q":"In one paragraph: what does a Kalman filter add over a complementary filter, and what does it cost on an MCU?"}]},{"n":"Control","subs":[{"n":"PID terms","t":"key","q":"Explain what P, I, and D each fix. Your system has steady-state error - which term? It oscillates - which term? It is noisy - which term is the problem?"},{"n":"Discretization","t":"key","q":"Write the discrete PID update. Why does the integral term need dt, and what breaks if your loop period jitters?"},{"n":"Integral windup and anti-windup","t":"key","q":"Saturate your actuator and let the integral grow. Show the overshoot on the plot. Now add clamping - show the fix."},{"n":"Derivative kick and D filtering","q":"Step the setpoint and watch D spike. Explain the cause. Fix it by taking D on the measurement, not the error."},{"n":"Tuning","q":"Tune a real PID (motor speed or temperature). Use manual tuning, then Ziegler-Nichols. Compare. Which gave a usable result faster?"},{"n":"Sample rate selection","q":"Your plant's time constant is 500 ms. Pick a PID loop rate and justify it. What happens if you run 10x faster than necessary?"}]}]},{"id":"pcb","num":"14","name":"PCB Literacy","month":"Folded into capstone | ~4 hrs","desc":"Read-only fluency, not a PCB role. Altium, Gerber export, DRC authoring, panelization and SI simulation are cut - that is PCB Design Engineer, a different job that costs 3 weeks you do not have.","topics":[{"n":"Reading a design","subs":[{"n":"Reading a schematic","t":"key","q":"Open the Nucleo schematic PDF. Identify: power entry, regulator, MCU, crystal, ST-LINK, and every connector. Explain each block aloud in 5 minutes."},{"n":"Reading a BOM and packages","q":"Identify 0402, 0603, SOIC, QFN, BGA by sight. Which can you hand-solder? Which needs a stencil and reflow?"},{"n":"DNP and assembly variants","t":"aw","q":"Find a 'do not populate' part on a real schematic. Why does the footprint exist if nothing goes there?"}]},{"n":"Stackup and planes","subs":[{"n":"2-layer vs 4-layer","t":"key","q":"Why is Sig/GND/PWR/Sig the standard 4-layer order? What does the inner ground plane give you that a 2-layer board cannot have at any cost?"},{"n":"Return current path","t":"key","q":"A high-speed trace runs over a split in the ground plane. Draw where the return current is forced to go. What did loop area just do to your EMI?"},{"n":"Never cross a split reference","t":"key","q":"Find one on a real board or a reference design. Explain the failure to an interviewer in three sentences."},{"n":"Decoupling placement","t":"key","q":"Why does a 100 nF cap 5 mm from the pin behave differently from one 1 mm away? Reason about loop inductance, not capacitance."}]},{"n":"Routing awareness","subs":[{"n":"Trace width vs current","t":"aw","q":"Your 3.3 V rail carries 1 A on an outer layer. Estimate the minimum trace width for a 10 degC rise. Which standard did you use?"},{"n":"Controlled impedance","t":"aw","q":"Why does a USB D+/D- pair need 90 ohm differential? What geometry sets it - name the four variables."},{"n":"Length matching","t":"aw","q":"When does length matching actually matter? Estimate the skew you can tolerate on a 480 Mbit/s pair."},{"n":"Crystal layout rules","t":"key","q":"Read the crystal layout section of your MCU's app note. List the three rules. Why is the guard ring there?"},{"n":"Antenna keepout","t":"have","q":"Find the keepout spec for an ESP32 module antenna. What happens to range if you pour ground under it?"},{"n":"Test points and fiducials","q":"Where would you add test points on your capstone board? What does a fiducial do, and who needs it?"},{"n":"ESD and TVS placement","q":"Where does the TVS go relative to the connector, and why does 5 mm matter?"}]}]},{"id":"dsa","num":"15","name":"DSA in C","month":"84 hrs | ~200 problems","desc":"Your React app names this domain 'Striver DSA (Hardware Specific)' and contains one topic. This is that domain, expanded. Write every solution in C - select C in the GfG IDE, not C++ or Python. Firmware rounds are watching you manage memory manually.","topics":[{"n":"Foundations","subs":[{"n":"Big-O, Theta, ohm","q":"State the complexity of: binary search, insertion sort best/worst, hash lookup average/worst. When does O(n^2) beat O(n log n) in practice?"},{"n":"Amortized analysis","q":"A dynamic array doubles on resize. Prove amortized O(1) append. Now explain why an MCU firmware would still ban it."},{"n":"Recursion depth on an MCU","t":"key","q":"Your task stack is 1 KB and each frame is 40 bytes. What recursion depth overflows it? Now argue why recursion is discouraged in firmware."}]},{"n":"Bit manipulation - Month 1","subs":[{"n":"Set, clear, toggle, test","t":"key","q":"Write all four for bit n. Then: given a uint32_t, clear bits 4 through 11 inclusive without touching the rest. One expression."},{"n":"n & (n-1)","t":"key","q":"Explain what it does bit-wise. Use it for: check power of two, and count set bits (Brian Kernighan). Why is Kernighan faster than a 32-iteration loop?"},{"n":"n & -n","q":"What does it isolate? Use it to find the lowest set bit's position. How does two's complement make this work?"},{"n":"Builtins: popcount, clz, ctz","q":"Rewrite your set-bit counter with __builtin_popcount. Compare cycles on Cortex-M4 with DWT. Did the compiler emit a single instruction?"},{"n":"XOR tricks","t":"key","q":"Array of n+1 numbers, one duplicate, find it with O(1) space. Then: every element appears twice except one - find it. Both with XOR."},{"n":"Find two single numbers","q":"Every element appears twice except two. Find both in O(n) time, O(1) space. Hint: partition by a differing bit."},{"n":"Reverse bits","q":"Reverse a 32-bit integer's bits. Do it naively, then in 5 operations using masks and shifts."},{"n":"Subsets via bitmask","q":"Generate all subsets of an n-element set using a bitmask loop. What is the complexity, and what is the practical n limit?"},{"n":"Round up to power of two","q":"Given n, find the next power of two >= n. Do it with shifts, then with __builtin_clz."}]},{"n":"Arrays and strings - Month 1","subs":[{"n":"Two pointers","t":"key","q":"Sorted array, find a pair summing to target - O(n), O(1) space. Then: remove duplicates in place and return the new length."},{"n":"Sliding window - fixed","q":"Maximum sum of any k consecutive elements. Then apply it: moving average over a live sensor stream with O(1) update."},{"n":"Sliding window - variable","t":"key","q":"Longest substring without repeating characters. Then: smallest subarray with sum >= target."},{"n":"Prefix sum","q":"Answer m range-sum queries in O(1) each after O(n) preprocessing. Then extend to a 2D matrix."},{"n":"Kadane's algorithm","q":"Maximum subarray sum in O(n). Then modify it to return the actual start and end indices."},{"n":"Dutch national flag","q":"Sort an array of 0s, 1s, 2s in one pass, O(1) space. Explain the three-pointer invariant."},{"n":"Merge intervals","q":"Merge overlapping intervals. What must you do first, and what does that do to the complexity?"},{"n":"C strings from scratch","t":"key","q":"Implement strlen, strcpy, strcmp, strrev, strstr - no standard library. Then handle the case strcpy gets wrong when buffers overlap."},{"n":"snprintf safety","t":"key","q":"What does snprintf return, and why is that value a trap? Write a safe append-to-buffer helper using it."},{"n":"atoi and itoa","q":"Implement both without library calls. Handle: leading spaces, sign, overflow. What does the real atoi do on overflow?"}]},{"n":"Searching and sorting - Month 2","subs":[{"n":"Binary search","t":"key","q":"Write it with no off-by-one. Then find first and last occurrence of a duplicated target. Then explain the `lo + (hi-lo)/2` overflow fix."},{"n":"Search in rotated array","t":"key","q":"Find a target in a rotated sorted array in O(log n). Explain how you decide which half is sorted."},{"n":"Binary search on answer","t":"key","q":"Given n piles and h hours, find the minimum eating speed. Explain why the answer space is monotonic and therefore searchable."},{"n":"Insertion sort - when it wins","q":"Sort 16 nearly-sorted elements with insertion sort and with quicksort. Time both. Why do real libraries switch to insertion sort below a threshold?"},{"n":"Merge sort","q":"Implement it. What is its space complexity, and why does that disqualify it for a 4 KB-RAM MCU?"},{"n":"Quicksort","t":"key","q":"Implement with Lomuto, then Hoare partition. Construct the input that makes it O(n^2). What does a real library do about it?"},{"n":"Stability","q":"Define a stable sort. Give a concrete case where stability changes the answer. Which of merge/quick/heap are stable?"},{"n":"qsort in C","q":"Sort a struct array by two keys using qsort with a comparator. What does the comparator return, and what breaks if you return `a-b` on large ints?"}]},{"n":"Stacks, queues, ring buffers - Month 2","subs":[{"n":"Stack from array and from list","q":"Implement both. Compare on: memory, cache behaviour, worst-case latency. Which belongs in firmware?"},{"n":"Balanced parentheses","q":"Validate a string of ()[]{}. Extend it to check a JSON payload's structural validity."},{"n":"Min stack","q":"Support push, pop, top, and getMin all in O(1). Explain the extra space, then optimize it."},{"n":"Monotonic stack","t":"key","q":"Next greater element for every position in O(n). Then: largest rectangle in a histogram. Explain the invariant."},{"n":"Queue from two stacks","q":"Implement it. What is the amortized complexity per operation, and why is that not the worst case?"},{"n":"Ring buffer","t":"key","q":"Implement with head/tail and power-of-two masking. Distinguish full from empty. Compare the 'waste one slot' trick against a separate count."},{"n":"Lock-free SPSC ring buffer","t":"key","q":"One producer (ISR), one consumer (main). Prove no lock is needed. Which variables must be volatile, and where does a memory barrier belong? You ship this for real in Week 8."},{"n":"Deque","q":"Implement a circular deque. Then use it for a sliding-window maximum in O(n)."}]},{"n":"Linked lists - Month 3","subs":[{"n":"Singly, doubly, circular","q":"Implement all three insert and delete. For each, state the one operation it makes O(1) that the others do not."},{"n":"Reverse iteratively","t":"key","q":"Reverse a singly linked list with three pointers, no recursion. Draw the pointer state after each iteration."},{"n":"Middle via slow-fast","t":"key","q":"Find the middle in one pass. For an even-length list, which middle do you return - and how do you control that?"},{"n":"Floyd cycle detection","t":"key","q":"Detect a cycle. Then find the cycle's start node. Prove why resetting one pointer to head works."},{"n":"Merge two sorted lists","q":"Merge in place with no extra nodes. Then extend to k lists - what data structure do you reach for?"},{"n":"Intersection of two lists","q":"Find the node where two lists merge, O(1) space. Explain the two-pointer length-equalizing trick."},{"n":"Palindrome linked list","q":"Check in O(n) time, O(1) space. You must reverse half. Then restore the list - why does that matter in production code?"},{"n":"Intrusive lists","t":"key","q":"Embed the list node inside the payload struct instead of pointing to it. Write container_of. This is how the Linux kernel and FreeRTOS do it - explain why."}]},{"n":"Hashing - Month 3","subs":[{"n":"Hash functions","q":"Implement FNV-1a. What makes a hash function good? Test yours for clustering on real key data."},{"n":"Chaining vs open addressing","t":"key","q":"Compare on: memory, cache behaviour, deletion complexity, worst case. Which suits a fixed-memory embedded system?"},{"n":"Load factor and resizing","q":"At what load factor do you resize, and by how much? Why does resizing disqualify hash tables from hard-real-time paths?"},{"n":"Implement a hash table in C","t":"key","q":"Full implementation: insert, lookup, delete, resize. No STL to hide behind. Then handle the deletion tombstone problem in open addressing."},{"n":"Two sum","q":"O(n) with a hash map. Then explain when the sorted two-pointer version is better despite being O(n log n)."},{"n":"Frequency map","q":"Find the top-k frequent elements. Which two data structures did you combine, and why?"}]},{"n":"Trees and heaps - Month 4","subs":[{"n":"Traversals - recursive and iterative","t":"key","q":"Write pre, in, and post order both ways. The iterative post-order is the hard one - do it with one stack."},{"n":"Level order / BFS","q":"Print level by level. Then print in zigzag. What data structure does each need?"},{"n":"Height and diameter","q":"Compute both in one pass. Why is the naive diameter solution O(n^2), and how does returning height fix it?"},{"n":"Lowest common ancestor","q":"Find LCA in a binary tree, then in a BST. Why is the BST version simpler?"},{"n":"BST insert, search, delete","t":"key","q":"Implement all three. The delete with two children is the real question - explain your choice of successor or predecessor."},{"n":"Validate a BST","t":"key","q":"The naive 'left < root < right' check is wrong. Construct the tree it wrongly accepts. Then write the correct version."},{"n":"Heap: heapify and build-heap","t":"key","q":"Implement a min-heap as an array. Prove build-heap is O(n), not O(n log n)."},{"n":"Top-K with a heap","q":"Find the k largest in a stream of n. Which heap type, and what size? Why is this better than sorting?"},{"n":"Timer priority queue","t":"key","q":"Design an RTOS delayed-task list as a min-heap of deadlines. What is the cost of inserting a new timer, and of firing the earliest? This is where heaps live in firmware."},{"n":"Trie","t":"aw","q":"Build a trie for AT-command prefix matching. Compare memory against a sorted array plus binary search."}]},{"n":"Recursion, graphs, DP - Month 5","subs":[{"n":"Backtracking: subsets and permutations","q":"Generate all subsets, then all permutations. State the complexity of each. Where does the recursion tree get pruned?"},{"n":"N-Queens","t":"aw","q":"Solve for n=8. What is your pruning strategy? How does a bitmask make it faster?"},{"n":"Graph representation","q":"Adjacency list vs matrix: compare memory and edge-lookup cost for a sparse graph of 10,000 nodes. Which do you pick?"},{"n":"BFS and DFS","t":"key","q":"Implement both. Which finds shortest path in an unweighted graph, and why does the other one not?"},{"n":"Topological sort","q":"Order tasks with dependencies. Do it with DFS, then with Kahn's algorithm. How does each detect a cycle?"},{"n":"Cycle detection","q":"Detect a cycle in a directed graph, then in an undirected one. Why do the two need different approaches?"},{"n":"DP: memo vs tabulation","q":"Solve Fibonacci both ways. Compare stack usage - which one can overflow an MCU stack?"},{"n":"Coin change","q":"Minimum coins for a target. Then count the number of ways. Explain why the loop order differs between the two."},{"n":"0/1 knapsack","q":"Solve it. Then reduce the space from O(nW) to O(W). Which loop direction did you have to reverse, and why?"},{"n":"Longest increasing subsequence","t":"aw","q":"Solve in O(n^2), then in O(n log n) with binary search. What does the auxiliary array actually hold - it is not the LIS."}]},{"n":"Embedded design set - Month 5","gap":1,"subs":[{"n":"LRU cache","t":"key","q":"O(1) get and put. Hash map plus doubly linked list. Build it. Then say where an LRU appears in firmware (hint: flash block cache)."},{"n":"Implement malloc","t":"key","q":"Write a free-list allocator over a static byte array: alloc, free, splitting, coalescing, alignment. Then demonstrate fragmentation in your own allocator."},{"n":"Fixed-size block allocator","t":"key","q":"32 blocks of 64 bytes, O(1) alloc and free. Prove fragmentation is structurally impossible. Why is this the firmware answer?"},{"n":"Bitmap allocator","q":"Track 256 blocks with a bit array. Find the first free block with __builtin_ctz. Compare speed against a free list."},{"n":"Object pool","q":"Pre-allocate N sensor-reading structs, hand them out and take them back. What happens on exhaustion, and who decides?"},{"n":"FSM dispatch table","t":"key","q":"Implement a protocol parser as a 2D table of function pointers indexed by [state][event]. Compare against switch-case on size and speed."},{"n":"Endian swap","t":"key","q":"Write swap16 and swap32. Then find the compiler builtin that does it in one instruction and prove it with disassembly."},{"n":"memcpy vs memmove","t":"key","q":"Implement both. Construct the overlapping case where your memcpy corrupts data and memmove does not. This is a real interview question."},{"n":"Aligned allocation","q":"Allocate a 32-byte-aligned DMA buffer from an unaligned pool. Show the pointer arithmetic and how you free it correctly."},{"n":"Circular log buffer with overwrite","q":"When full, overwrite the oldest entry. Handle variable-length records. What happens if a reader is mid-record when the writer wraps?"},{"n":"Debounce state machine","q":"Implement a debouncer as an explicit FSM, not a delay. Why is delay() wrong here, and what does your FSM cost per tick?"}]},{"n":"Interview craft","subs":[{"n":"The seven-step method","t":"key","q":"Clarify -> examples -> brute force -> optimize -> state complexity -> code -> dry run. Apply it out loud to one medium problem, timed at 30 minutes."},{"n":"Edge case reflex","t":"key","q":"Build your standing checklist: empty, single element, all same, overflow, NULL, negative, max size. Apply it to three past solutions - how many broke?"},{"n":"Timed mocks","q":"Do 4 mocks: 30 minutes, one problem, spoken aloud, in C. Record yourself. Watch it back. What did you not say that you should have?"}]}]},{"id":"soft","num":"16","name":"Soft Skills and Getting Hired","month":"Woven throughout","desc":"Zero coverage in my earlier drafts, and a real gap - the m3y54m roadmap is right to give it a whole branch. You are job-hunting, and none of the 661 rows above matter if you cannot explain them to another person. These are still practice questions: do them out loud, and record yourself.","topics":[{"n":"Working with other people","subs":[{"n":"Talking to a hardware engineer","t":"key","q":"The board is dead. Write the actual message you would send: what you measured, what you have ruled out, what you need from them. No blame, no guessing, no 'it doesn't work'."},{"n":"Writing a bug report","t":"key","q":"Write one for a bug you really hit this week: expected, actual, steps to reproduce, environment, what you already tried. Hand it to someone else - can they reproduce it without asking you anything?"},{"n":"Code review etiquette","q":"Review a peer's pull request. Write three comments: one blocking, one suggestion, one question. Re-read them as if you were the author. Which one lands badly?"},{"n":"Estimation, and being honest about it","t":"key","q":"Estimate your next subtopic in hours before starting. Log the actual. Do this ten times and compute your personal multiplier. Most engineers are near 2x - what are you?"},{"n":"Saying 'I don't know'","t":"key","q":"Rehearse the sentence: 'I don't know - here is how I would find out.' Use it at least once in your next mock interview. Bluffing is the fastest way to fail a firmware round."},{"n":"Asking a good question","q":"Before asking for help, write: what I am trying to do, what I tried, what happened, what I think is going on. Notice how often writing this answers it."}]},{"n":"Getting hired","subs":[{"n":"Reading a job description honestly","t":"key","q":"Take a real embedded job ad. Mark every requirement: have it / can learn in a week / cannot. Apply if you have about 60%. What did you mark 'cannot', and is it in this syllabus?"},{"n":"The 90-second project pitch","t":"key","q":"Pitch your capstone in 90 seconds: the problem, one design decision, the trade-off you accepted, the result. Record it on your phone. Watch it back. Cut the first 20 seconds."},{"n":"The portfolio README","t":"key","q":"A recruiter gives your repo 30 seconds. Does the first paragraph say what it does, on what hardware, and why it was hard? Open your own repo and time yourself."},{"n":"'Tell me about a bug you fixed'","t":"key","q":"Prepare one story: symptom, your hypotheses, how you narrowed it down, the root cause, the fix, and what you changed afterwards so it could not recur. The last part is what they are listening for."},{"n":"Whiteboard behaviour","q":"In your next mock, narrate every step out loud. Then watch the recording with the sound off - could someone follow your thinking from the board alone?"},{"n":"Keeping a work log","q":"Log one line a day: what you did, what broke, what you learned. After a month, read it. That log is where your interview stories come from - you will not remember them otherwise."}]},{"n":"Where the jobs are - India","subs":[{"n":"Automotive - the largest embedded employer here","t":"key","q":"Bosch, Continental, Valeo, Aptiv, Tata Elxsi, Harman. Open ONE real embedded ad from any of them. Which does it name: CAN, AUTOSAR, ISO 26262, MISRA C? Find each in Sections 5 and 12. How many can you do today, honestly?"},{"n":"Semiconductor - they make the chips you program","t":"key","q":"Qualcomm, Texas Instruments, NXP, Infineon, Microchip, STMicro. These rounds go deepest into Section 2 (Cortex-M) and Section 3 (registers, not HAL). Open one of their ads and count how many Section 2 rows it implies. That is your revision list."},{"n":"Consumer electronics","q":"Samsung R&D, OnePlus/OPPO, Xiaomi India, Logitech, Bose, Zebronics. Consumer means battery life and BLE - Sections 10 and 11. State your capstone's MEASURED battery life in months, with the current numbers behind it. That number is the interview."},{"n":"Defence and aerospace","q":"DRDO, ISRO, HAL, L&T Defence, BEL, Bharat Forge. Highest reliability; DO-178C governs airborne software (Section 12). Before you spend a week on an application, check the citizenship and clearance requirements. Do that first, not last."},{"n":"Medical devices","q":"Life-critical, zero failure tolerance. Which standard governs medical device software (Section 12)? Name one thing it demands that your capstone does not currently have."},{"n":"Industrial and IoT - your home turf","t":"have","q":"Smart meters, SCADA, factory sensors. This is exactly your IIITH Wi-SUN and oneM2M deployment. Write the two sentences that connect that work to a smart-metering ad. If you cannot, you are wasting your single strongest card."},{"n":"Your sector decides which standard you must know","t":"key","q":"Match them: ISO 26262, IEC 62304, DO-178C, IEC 61508 to automotive, medical, airborne, general industrial. Now: which sector are you targeting, and have you ticked its standard in Section 12?"},{"n":"Pick ONE sector before you apply","t":"key","q":"Choose one sector and one company. Open the ad. Mark every line have / learn-in-a-week / cannot. If something is missing from this syllabus entirely, that is a real gap - tell me and I will add it."},{"n":"Embedded vs VLSI vs CS - your target","q":"Embedded is C/C++ at board and OS level. VLSI is Verilog/SystemVerilog at RTL and transistor level. Your main target is embedded and hardware roles, where your projects already win. VLSI is your second target, which is why Section V (Digital Design and Verilog) now exists. For each company you apply to, say in one line which of the two it is and which sections to revise.","t":"key"},{"n":"Read career content critically","t":"key","q":"A roadmap PDF told you '50 billion devices by 2030'. Trace it: that is Ericsson's 2010 prediction for 2020 - which Ericsson themselves cut to about 26 billion, and Gartner put at 20.8 billion. It never happened; someone just moved the date. Run this check on every statistic before you repeat it in an interview."},{"n":"Salary claims in career content","q":"Those PDFs quote CTC bands with no source, no city, no experience level, no year. Treat them as noise. Get real numbers instead: ask seniors in the actual company, and check AmbitionBox / Glassdoor / Levels.fyi for a specific role, company and city. Never negotiate off an Instagram graphic."}]}]}];
